#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "590953b8-2533-5a4b-bf4f-f002b0367919" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Vector in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a7c078d-19ef-546c-aac2-8965df8b5900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391792} {:answer/id #uuid "17dfda34-666b-58e7-95c0-b80675d1c00f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391793} {:answer/id #uuid "58d99fc6-7b1d-5ec4-8b66-4dc4564dfcbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391794} {:answer/id #uuid "95f4185d-53f9-500f-aa63-d2fdf30556e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391795} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5469f2-4e80-52c6-9765-adf91fc0519f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a7c078d-19ef-546c-aac2-8965df8b5900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391792} :answer-field/id #uuid "d42b67bc-bf40-52c3-9b93-8c498d0d9ad8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ca48b5a-ff7c-5739-a4dc-d774d869fa76" :question/math-academy-id "q-34247" :question/problem "Find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$ given that $3a+2x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+2x & =b \\\\ 2x & =b-3a \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}(b-3a) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6+7=13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24069f64-c580-5200-b5e0-1c7ef2ae7e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315321} {:answer/id #uuid "3a15928d-e29a-514a-aa9c-b81e928ccc22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315451} {:answer/id #uuid "6b92d802-3085-528a-9c72-efc57cf5c2c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315724} {:answer/id #uuid "800f5135-a705-5bc5-b182-cd0428d00e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315821} {:answer/id #uuid "93c3185c-8c40-5110-addf-07d04037df9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a15928d-e29a-514a-aa9c-b81e928ccc22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315451} :answer-field/id #uuid "22bb0e27-d6d9-5966-9fb5-317681ddbc7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1120ac93-1146-5212-b323-41cae9ac4014" :question/math-academy-id "q-34276" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252d775f-77ed-511b-95ad-524d7bb4cd39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315326} {:answer/id #uuid "6bcab351-5c37-5d1b-99d8-985db29eacd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315726} {:answer/id #uuid "969cb243-da0c-5f4c-8e30-aedc2baa4e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants III and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315954} {:answer/id #uuid "ae942c7b-d556-576d-99b4-c2d5e0d5ad8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316110} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b0d548-2819-5f8e-b792-8ccfa0fb789c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "969cb243-da0c-5f4c-8e30-aedc2baa4e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants III and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315954} :answer-field/id #uuid "e94ae561-6bac-5708-8ab7-f7ca8354b9fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8483a20c-9e8e-5588-94ee-dd0e0014806e" :question/math-academy-id "q-34325" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-{450}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{450}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{450}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-90$\n\n- $-{450}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={270}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${270}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{450}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/35/35b9783c9ac87b0a8d177e7bdf340b48513146404b7c768a8532da59b6ec7948.png)\n\nWe see that ${270}^{∘}$ lies between quadrants III and IV. Therefore, $-{450}^{∘}$ also lies between these quadrants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db2971e6-1fc2-5a15-b561-63195b136074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385278} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0fa27a-ed94-5c90-a481-896ad99baa7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{Q(x)}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385279} {:answer/id #uuid "7c03e432-04c5-58b2-bf0b-dbaba725fc42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{Q(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385280} {:answer/id #uuid "63a60a7b-ecc2-5e4a-858a-caf48320bd97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63a60a7b-ecc2-5e4a-858a-caf48320bd97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385281} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4ae3c95-790e-5efb-bded-f1630a881644" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385277} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2564f937-4e1b-55d8-a807-4d2d69e01a18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385283} {:answer/id #uuid "9f83c1f7-9351-5f02-bca9-c83c0178d49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385284} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f98a71-e54b-50f5-a520-00700a34623b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385285} {:answer/id #uuid "485bf278-ab89-556a-ba05-f21c26edb3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "485bf278-ab89-556a-ba05-f21c26edb3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385286} :answer-field/id #uuid "276087f8-befa-5298-8a1a-080955d42715" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385282} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad9ff701-1597-5963-80fd-d658b7088388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385288} {:answer/id #uuid "3376d197-15e8-50c5-a82f-7f0518430a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385289} {:answer/id #uuid "9da40d53-dced-51c6-9ebb-9faebb28b864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad9ff701-1597-5963-80fd-d658b7088388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385288} :answer-field/id #uuid "d433757c-272d-5127-8bc9-136e8756866c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "736a842a-a005-5f7c-a75f-80d3e3e641b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-343302" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=2.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=2,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)} \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7f04ab7-f6f6-5d01-bfcf-b667e37e4f0b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Irrational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "824db7a0-468b-5b7c-bdd3-7bd19185dd56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 80, z =-5 ± 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066779} {:answer/id #uuid "f44af2a5-1591-5f5e-93ba-40c0ddd584b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 80, z =-8 ± 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066780} {:answer/id #uuid "30dfca08-4199-59da-8d02-2600e0f2122b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 75, z = 5 ± 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066781} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3b9749-