{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c0076f5-08a5-5d4c-9789-196650ab8295" :question/math-academy-id "q-3435" :question/problem "The equation ${z}^{2}-50=-10z$ can be written in the form $(z+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $-10z$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-50 & =-10z \\\\ {z}^{2}+10z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2} & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=5,q=75,$ and $p+q=80.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2} & =75 \\\\ \\sqrt{(z+5{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ |z+5| & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ z+5 & =\\pm 5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ z & =-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1000f1-7874-54ec-a25c-a6fdd4c99c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058255} {:answer/id #uuid "3d05c2d3-5f8b-520c-9698-93cd44016078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058256} {:answer/id #uuid "3e675e47-ccc8-5f07-88dd-4486ea14690b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058257} {:answer/id #uuid "4077c45f-7284-5f8a-b112-9435133409d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058258} {:answer/id #uuid "73d4409a-0928-587b-a8b0-c3b59584d36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058259}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ba6c658f-3e45-5acf-b1fd-cc6fc99efea5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058254} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f053f6c2-d473-5f4a-9761-a34e3f8fa5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402370} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c1d7ef-187c-5c23-afd5-1de27fa281b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402371} {:answer/id #uuid "e2833382-6b90-5b3c-ae32-77694f3ceaef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402372} {:answer/id #uuid "79867f36-2c63-53a2-b0d7-906e247536ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402373} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1cfc22-b026-5ac0-aed5-a3a643bfff05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2833382-6b90-5b3c-ae32-77694f3ceaef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402372} :answer-field/id #uuid "88bbe855-4ccb-5da3-a31c-9c9bb8b0b176" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80f5801f-10e6-5373-bb84-2300dad1ed58" :question/math-academy-id "q-34403" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/89/891959642b0d055d852329c59be7541179dd5ae0c4182c2bec533261ce3a700d.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33586620-e110-5a37-874f-a92103c5e9fb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for One Coefficient in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31be8d1f-3f88-58ee-bd33-1775efcf7376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317721} {:answer/id #uuid "68f02f55-adcf-52bf-a9c0-303e07dff714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317924} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0a721f-8c97-5595-84ac-09f5e1a54ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318033} {:answer/id #uuid "bb771e3e-bdfb-53b0-8d4d-91cebbd855e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318187} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9e6693-364a-5d56-8ace-d64fc0ed7b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb771e3e-bdfb-53b0-8d4d-91cebbd855e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318187} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9b90697-9d45-5745-8d5a-d193273739f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8dd7df5-a62a-5efa-a6ef-0c480856813f" :question/math-academy-id "q-34600" :question/problem "Find $l$, if $5{a}_{1}+l{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $l$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}+l{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 5[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+l[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\ l[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ l[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that \n$l=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078568 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d40eb58-2655-5469-940e-f80c613a366a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397725} {:answer/id #uuid "cd504e90-e5b7-56c6-964b-40f89c7b3493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397726} {:answer/id #uuid "a07cbcd9-56d0-586c-aa21-1a80b6af5ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397727} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc20b50-1f58-592c-8612-6b49865b0664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397728} {:answer/id #uuid "286353c9-a32e-56eb-8992-b6c84200e329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a07cbcd9-56d0-586c-aa21-1a80b6af5ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397727} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a9687f0-3dbc-5f03-bc8c-36c768da7028" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92b8f43d-f4d5-509d-8b12-68825b47ce0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397731} {:answer/id #uuid "ef65b10b-e65d-5550-b964-4477632fe18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92b8f43d-f4d5-509d-8b12-68825b47ce0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397731} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b792d3-0847-501a-907c-2ebe956b9ca4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb9ed8ae-2017-567d-8d5e-a0f446be0601" :question/math-academy-id "q-346720" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(0,2)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-2,2).$ If ${f}^{′}(0)=0.5,$ estimate the value of $f(0.4)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.$\\,f(0.4)≈$ {{field-1}} and the approximation is an {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(0,2),$ so we have $f(0)=2.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(0)=0.5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+0.5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(0.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=0.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.4) & ≈L(0.4) \\\\  & =2+0.5(0.4) \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-2,2),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=0,$ and therefore we have an $\\text{overestimate}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fafc8c8-0452-582f-b603-6f633de9a39a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of Two Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f8e453-5b4e-5424-8df3-89ef36cde644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317752} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9175b7-617f-57a3-af8d-919ed2b0db92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317756} {:answer/id #uuid "8e503817-bf60-566b-9a9f-488b451dfc84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318048} {:answer/id #uuid "d442f1af-21b6-52b3-b5fa-b7866251aa30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318271} {:answer/id #uuid "e402728a-9968-5173-84f1-7ebaf6de023f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39f8e453-5b4e-5424-8df3-89ef36cde644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317752} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e636a51-398f-568c-9040-f35409919a73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9bdc8c8-a628-5a28-9c53-49f974293c50" :question/math-academy-id "q-3471" :question/problem "The curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ intersects the line $4y-5x=8$ at the points $(p,q)$ and $(r,s).$ What is $p+q+r+s?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we solve for $y$ in the second curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-5x & =8 \\\\ 4y & =5x+8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y=\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{5x+8}{4} \\\\ 4 & =5{x}^{2}+8x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+8x-4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(5x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-2$ and $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{2}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nFinally, if we let $p=-2,q=-\\frac{1}{2},r=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $s=\\frac{5}{2},$ then\n\n$$\np+q+r+s=-2-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d995288e573a22db67c7d4c82c1a12bcbb41fd880f706b9fb62062aa90e2d5ba.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b31f2bdc-c267-5fc7-bd0f-283777ee4fee" :knowledge-point/title "The Derivative of a Three-Component Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d8f4b31-7cd4-567a-b1ab-e1908f4c5bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+{e}^{\\pi }\\,j-2{e}^{\\pi }\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391892} {:answer/id #uuid "770c4032-8926-5b49-96b8-17c721eb3eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }\\,i-{e}^{\\pi }\\,j+{e}^{\\pi }\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391893} {:answer/id #uuid "d767d08e-9f3d-5075-804a-e32b3b238995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391894} {:answer/id #uuid "53ba9a50-f890-5647-8a42-ad787ec3b6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+{e}^{\\pi }\\,j-{e}^{\\pi }\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391895} {:answer/id #uuid "fadb0885-54d3-518c-85c6-6d456aea62cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{\\pi }\\,i-{e}^{\\pi }\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53ba9a50-f890-5647-8a42-ad787ec3b6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+{e}^{\\pi }\\,j-{e}^{\\pi }\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391895} :answer-field/id #uuid "1620165b-3bcb-5548-8049-48ba8d5cc382" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e579ce0-d5f9-57b8-931d-2fc18fc335d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-34724" :question/problem "Find the derivative of  $f(t)={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k$ at $t=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute ${f}^{′}(\\pi )$ we substitute $t=\\pi $ into the expression for ${f}^{′}(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =({e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )\\,i+{e}^{\\pi }\\,j+({e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -{e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0+{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-1))\\,i+{e}^{\\pi }\\,j+({e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-1)-{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0)\\,k \\\\  & =-{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+{e}^{\\pi }\\,j-{e}^{\\pi }\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53025447-2eb1-5df5-a00f-6e8c8a103513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{89}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319630} {:answer/id #uuid "66db5717-8d6c-56e3-9e6e-8159327ca744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{67}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319706} {:answer/id #uuid "6f875b91-463b-5b9e-b642-9334220d3e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319727} {:answer/id #uuid "78093d05-d766-51e5-bfbd-15a4b1ad83e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{67}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319753} {:answer/id #uuid "b538e599-0c29-5367-95db-1d80a51d71e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b538e599-0c29-5367-95db-1d80a51d71e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319948} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c1dc974-537c-5a19-8b0e-de4ef3bf427a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b2f4df1-3e4e-5541-bb2f-d2b7890aaf73" :question/math-academy-id "q-35079" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2{z}^{2}-14z+16$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({z}^{2}+7z-8)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+7z-8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z-8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({z}^{2}+7z-8) & =-2({(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105854 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ff9d00-cfe3-5000-9f39-f5b084d00080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319455} {:answer/id #uuid "9035921e-6fcf-5772-b9a5-7d9a496b6106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319823} {:answer/id #uuid "d6df4228-d351-594e-9eea-e865f3a883bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320046} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc970a1-4ea5-589f-8352-ce90a07452c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320063} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a74ebb-b1db-5cf0-a20e-5165336efc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6df4228-d351-594e-9eea-e865f3a883bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320046} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cb04ca6-e219-5d5e-ab67-487f7d66d988" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c7529a5-2ffe-53f0-9bca-cd9b5d97a802" :question/math-academy-id "q-35192" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{5}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-\\frac{1}{4}$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5} & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4})=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40a70c8d-dc8e-52bd-a679-ffd6d1940451" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Function With a Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9b836c-fae2-50e0-a795-3a27cfdd5785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+a}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319421} {:answer/id #uuid "3492000c-c2fa-51a4-bcee-24c414d9ca52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=7x-a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319554} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3d6dcf-2ec9-5287-a8ef-e92f9f472281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-7}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319812} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb04d9c-7b29-5181-b95e-06fc65228ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+7}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320017} {:answer/id #uuid "d00e46ee-e183-5993-a320-dcbaa9aa4eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-a}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccb04d9c-7b29-5181-b95e-06fc65228ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+7}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320017} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5d4e30f-32ac-5460-b456-38e5a87775f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "abfc488e-a974-5dbc-b40c-2f281295336b" :question/math-academy-id "q-35217" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=ax-7$, where $a$ is a non-zero constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=ax-7\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=ay-7\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =ay-7 \\\\ x+7 & =ay \\\\ \\frac{x+7}{a} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+7}{a}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ccc7bce-8234-5ee1-bd8b-2bcdd3fcbd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319574} {:answer/id #uuid "574ba857-9f96-590d-abdc-bb096d802847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319648} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7a7fe3-5133-5ba3-baf2-2c6b2e00ce1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319916} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c8a647-b5b3-5af4-85b7-661bdc068c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320074} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b296d9-adc9-527b-a4b4-ccaabe649e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ccc7bce-8234-5ee1-bd8b-2bcdd3fcbd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319574} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e14ff5e-6a4f-5e24-8876-c8a24ddf7181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ffa1d84-420d-51e5-b5d5-2102976566da" :question/math-academy-id "q-35243" :question/problem "![](images/67/67b77d2d3798e87593c7dddeea93603fff36d9153f53248cc2dab295f4879321.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule and the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x))}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{3}$ does not change the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2})={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40a70c8d-dc8e-52bd-a679-ffd6d1940451" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Function With a Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28a8e3f3-e6e5-59bd-9144-f9733223829f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319519} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee8eb52-9b9f-56f1-9ecb-13190d21a579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319819} {:answer/id #uuid "b056fbea-f31f-565e-a6ee-a22a4a856c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319927} {:answer/id #uuid "b321e6e4-fc1e-54d2-9a1a-305a18b80a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax+11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319939} {:answer/id #uuid "b495768c-d594-5351-a058-aad82b451854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=x+11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28a8e3f3-e6e5-59bd-9144-f9733223829f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319519} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5fa981-8f9f-5e72-b3b9-955186ce41e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40530ca3-e238-5640-94b0-1433afa01d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35310" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{a}+11$, where $a$ is a non-zero constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{a}+11\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y}{a}+11\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{y}{a}+11 \\\\ x-11 & =\\frac{y}{a} \\\\ a(x-11) & =y \\\\ ax-11a & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11a\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185960eb-bfc4-5b04-9b8e-889e9fa563fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402404} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e16aa5-8900-58f3-8772-5eddc1069f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402405} {:answer/id #uuid "de3fec5a-67b7-579c-a809-55a0c1f4c2ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402406} {:answer/id #uuid "10adf48f-3c4e-533f-ba78-f17c436ead07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402407} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0475d5-57f8-5642-b3fc-c1fb1233e0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7e16aa5-8900-58f3-8772-5eddc1069f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402405} :answer-field/id #uuid "181f4699-3530-5c7f-a1cf-43e09f53ac45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da57a6bc-d0db-59f4-81d7-c54fef1172ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-35379" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3).$" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\,(\\infty ) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124478 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5f0d2e9-1cf6-5fe4-85cc-f401382f39b2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Non-Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8779bf35-546f-5765-bb56-68f1c9880ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121881} {:answer/id #uuid "ada3b0df-752c-5e3e-9f3e-cce77acb7c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121882} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ce2854-fd1a-5255-bf12-736a9db6d465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121883} {:answer/id #uuid "3aaf0356-bea4-57dc-94dc-d0a8943c4b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121884} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca98bf6-3fde-56af-8234-c7a3b296a200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121885}] :answer-field/id #uuid "381a65ce-186a-52ec-b16a-760c7868d6d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121880} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f29001e-1914-5f99-b0d4-2b79d02e6047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368477} {:answer/id #uuid "64624a3d-dfdd-5307-b92c-12954ac9ef44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368478} {:answer/id #uuid "4b55be80-4783-555a-ab11-127f769351b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368479} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d1676f-4ae2-5d22-a783-84704327b1ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368480} {:answer/id #uuid "13f58a79-b05a-5fe7-afc8-12e30d537ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f29001e-1914-5f99-b0d4-2b79d02e6047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368477} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ea66d08-2ce2-54bc-b37d-f9e1be771b2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfb0514f-33ea-5674-8071-994ec564a12b" :question/math-academy-id "q-35391" :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $4$ pivots in the $1$st, $2$nd, $4$th, and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bdb6bc9-47e2-5330-a1ee-270882f2aced" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Statements Using the Vector Space Axioms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4f1d1a-384a-5b9f-b52c-026a37aea598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A3}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402436} {:answer/id #uuid "7be29340-c311-570d-a8a5-d715d90e7b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402437} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ffdc61-92b7-525f-ba13-ec7d8e5bece2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A7}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402438} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb894df-460c-5d88-98ab-47811b8def6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402439} {:answer/id #uuid "27104a21-fa7a-5b79-b863-dfef8c36d3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A5}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3ffdc61-92b7-525f-ba13-ec7d8e5bece2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A7}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402438} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a7cbdf1-0f6c-51d1-a347-9dc567706a31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cf85bb24-d080-5608-9b8e-8c02ff8974d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-35559" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement: *\n\n*$0x=0$ for any $x\\in V$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (0+0)x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{property of number }0\\,] \\\\ 0x+0x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ (0x+0x)+(-0x) & =0x+(-0x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-0x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (0x+0x)+(-0x) & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0x+(0x+(-0x)) & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ 0x+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0x & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (0+0)x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{property of number }0\\,] \\\\ 0x+0x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A7}\\,] \\\\ (0x+0x)+(-0x) & =0x+(-0x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-0x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (0x+0x)+(-0x) & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0x+(0x+(-0x)) & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ 0x+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0x & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124480 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a557d6-52f3-5df3-8088-5b875296e72e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40487040-b1c7-5407-a2db-530c13d29890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321655} {:answer/id #uuid "4a91e3df-5714-5e10-9d58-d3f745be7029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321733} {:answer/id #uuid "8070b2ca-160e-522b-88cd-4b488700eb85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322158} {:answer/id #uuid "c668b67a-678d-5d3f-841c-696ee5529a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322684} {:answer/id #uuid "df88605e-821c-52cd-b219-da4d542b424b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8070b2ca-160e-522b-88cd-4b488700eb85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322158} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb224766-b6eb-5fc3-b8b4-8f8e41f498a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bdb5ab47-cea8-5da9-89b4-45a9046d7661" :question/math-academy-id "q-35734" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $B$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124663 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "349d6fca-cfd5-5899-97f9-619f38ffc399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321547} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6ce2ac-2a25-54b2-839c-b1cc6290b561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322759} {:answer/id #uuid "d539cb73-cc46-54a7-951a-365d9f621a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322807} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c15bef-666c-5440-98b0-35f3376a287f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322920} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb9dd70-fd1d-55b4-8e19-6849ed66d65b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "349d6fca-cfd5-5899-97f9-619f38ffc399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321547} :answer-field/id #uuid "d568f8c0-9a4c-5bd9-a0d2-159cca6bb4c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24c1ddd3-5086-5a99-bc61-14c84e2545e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35763" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+5}{2{x}^{3}+2x+6}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x+5}{2{x}^{3}+2x+6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{2+0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124664 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2842cc73-0f01-58d5-b5f8-0139583e8db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321461} {:answer/id #uuid "2de3d6aa-490c-5cae-a784-6e667b895471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321506} {:answer/id #uuid "a00a0949-433b-5624-94b0-a6603a842920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322412} {:answer/id #uuid "a251689c-7067-57e5-bcb5-7bc51fb827e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322428} {:answer/id #uuid "b425d295-6b7a-560e-97ee-8069726e7fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b425d295-6b7a-560e-97ee-8069726e7fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322555} :answer-field/id #uuid "bad8f0e9-eadc-541a-b41e-da64a5a6afcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed660c39-3116-519a-a531-99463ad097b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35833" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-9x+1}{x+4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{2}-9x+1}{x+4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{x}-\\frac{9x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\frac{x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x-9+\\frac{1}{x})}{(1+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x-9+0)}{(1+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4x)-9 \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124664 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "544fe8c0-00d7-5582-9b43-a7537bdb2cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321810} {:answer/id #uuid "8d44af16-7047-5688-8bee-4018f24233a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322274} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f05e28-da65-5e0b-9cd3-41729a024a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322534} {:answer/id #uuid "b324732c-ba06-52ac-9465-f950f027ee85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322547} {:answer/id #uuid "d8824ca2-db5e-5819-9375-3a960341c205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "544fe8c0-00d7-5582-9b43-a7537bdb2cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321810} :answer-field/id #uuid "1017af27-6db1-5bfd-9bf5-b120516afb73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5a61e3c-7091-5d14-ae25-993daf76ef12" :question/math-academy-id "q-35834" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{12x-3{x}^{4}}{3{x}^{2}+7}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{12x-3{x}^{4}}{3{x}^{2}+7} \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{12x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{12}{x}-3{x}^{2})}{(3+\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{0-3{x}^{2}}{3+0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068855 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6e4cf3-803a-5942-9ce0-7047942e078a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsine Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb97f34-32f2-5fbb-aec5-57ace0fbaad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321872} {:answer/id #uuid "8b638a94-901f-5e77-bbce-f68bb55836a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x-4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322260} {:answer/id #uuid "c656713f-8a67-5fd6-8734-5401564b9a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x+4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322683} {:answer/id #uuid "d689d4b8-8b89-5de7-b04a-9ad318e85b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322822} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7edee3-ab4b-5148-91d5-eeb3f01579ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c656713f-8a67-5fd6-8734-5401564b9a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x+4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322683} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a9b73dc-645a-581e-bb60-7a672cb6e2c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84868fd8-3ce0-5bf2-96b8-b86160226a86" :question/math-academy-id "q-3594" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-8x-15}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-8x-15 & =-({x}^{2}+8x+15) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+[{4}^{2}-{4}^{2}]+15) \\\\  & =-((x+4)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-8x-15}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+4).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-8x-15}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x+4)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcf425bc-d6ce-5cab-87ea-2d4d42270d09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of a Line Given a Point and Direction Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23fbaa09-de32-5279-8c1f-f239b074a63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,3,3⟩+t⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-5,3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321424} {:answer/id #uuid "7c0e6aad-38ab-52d1-81b7-7b6c73dd7206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-5,3⟩+t⟨-3,3,3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322122} {:answer/id #uuid "ab82beab-bc5f-593a-9794-96e63dcdf6ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,-3,3⟩+t⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},3,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322492} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4c1e66-fed9-5a5d-ae1f-bb20400224bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-5,3,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}⟩+t⟨3,3,-3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322615} {:answer/id #uuid "ea402ada-1a50-5d82-91e1-f13ae4ee687f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,3,3⟩+⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-5,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23fbaa09-de32-5279-8c1f-f239b074a63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,3,3⟩+t⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-5,3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321424} :answer-field/id #uuid "85b9ac94-59f8-5567-aad5-41fed7d45a8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11b71ac6-a4d7-52a1-a6f2-cd7bc01c5f0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-35965" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q(-3,3,3)$ and parallel to $v=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-5,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=q+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $Q$ is $q=⟨-3,3,3⟩.$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨-3,3,3⟩+t⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-5,3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7739ff43-c68c-58ca-8bbe-3cf898ab85f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322081} {:answer/id #uuid "8d033687-0dda-5d24-a50d-81609e131df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322273} {:answer/id #uuid "8f052679-93df-563a-8c3a-49901beaf19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322283} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2a112f-a59c-5195-8734-7545ee263058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322736} {:answer/id #uuid "eefd406f-e3a9-51c3-9df4-f6d2a447cae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eefd406f-e3a9-51c3-9df4-f6d2a447cae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323031} :answer-field/id #uuid "661e0d23-dac0-58fe-aeb6-82bf0e4b60b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "544516cf-cdec-5de8-af07-1ff7e5be101e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36004" :question/problem "![](images/15/1597ad53471d0d9b44812c5caa31bcb160cfa150d1df9a9314447fa49e62ccff.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in [-6,0]$\n\nII. $x\\in [-6,4)$\n\nIII. $x\\in [4,10)$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in [-6,0]$ and $x\\in [4,10).$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/7c/7cb282e3fa66af801ff03816973d5f5f1d0cb41e39d08a8addd27d7aa9c5b9df.png)\n\n      \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $[-6,4]$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/00/000953609a7b3e13ad09473ead62f96aa895c7f94aa618884e69883595004fd4.png)\n\n \n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I  and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "014f6668-666f-5040-a144-715105254126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321160} {:answer/id #uuid "26c34079-b0a6-5fc3-9a77-ee93f052ff20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321452} {:answer/id #uuid "80353f56-f611-551a-b4e9-abf19a92d1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322153} {:answer/id #uuid "968df320-cd19-54ac-b971-d412835fd3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322348} {:answer/id #uuid "f59bf802-b4bc-54f8-8889-7338410f2cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80353f56-f611-551a-b4e9-abf19a92d1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322153} :answer-field/id #uuid "28b86894-2e1c-5167-ac3e-a4dd5b6514f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d31db013-e3f7-5fe2-aefa-8428da418673" :question/math-academy-id "q-36011" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b02c2e94e144935f0ff96846cd51b5a969fa8f884a7ca1d09abb231b7c945232.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible? \n\nI. $x\\in (-4,-2)$\n\nII. $x\\in [-2,3]$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-3,3]$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-4,-2)$ and $x\\in [-2,3].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/53/53fe88780b2ccb7522d2f1baa711162ec99300c2a2d4da29bba9ca532026cd07.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on  the interval $x\\in [-3,3]$  because some lines will  intersect the curve at more than one point.\n\n![](images/63/63cee4fc05ab17a8e69d9bfae578fa83c24d73e4677065a54a33a25e0871e718.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124498 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f232aed9-1584-5e57-a881-226a6876384a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Straight Line Given Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "440ff2d6-6a57-592f-890a-cb36c4030b99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402483} {:answer/id #uuid "1b273b92-8a6a-58c5-87d5-d8bd24c489a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402484} {:answer/id #uuid "dd66cfbb-7b6d-5d5f-953d-d76860d416ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402485} {:answer/id #uuid "9d828f3b-5ac7-5510-af91-3c48513bcd3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402486} {:answer/id #uuid "adf2184f-2ab7-5a41-899d-7af938d2d615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adf2184f-2ab7-5a41-899d-7af938d2d615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402487} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ab63e7a-abce-576e-813c-95b8e9f02934" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9efb0092-3045-58e2-bed4-ca88fc8fe6c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-36023" :question/problem "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=5 \\\\ y=4t \\\\ z=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat would be the vector equation of the line?" :question/worked-solution "These parametric equations are equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4t \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07090fc8-c92c-5618-9012-cf0f1646450c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321202} {:answer/id #uuid "0fe1e716-191b-5261-a643-11dccd52c0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321272} {:answer/id #uuid "7058d824-9a65-5aad-a618-ec1279c82cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322025} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8f2599-68f2-5e06-b402-e4e77d2f9558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322263} {:answer/id #uuid "c5538761-3933-52cf-bf3a-e8775a2d4878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7058d824-9a65-5aad-a618-ec1279c82cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322025} :answer-field/id #uuid "2700a4d9-d130-5eeb-a944-7092bfe21365" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8099f21-9137-5121-a075-a61a4c5ae84b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36039" :question/problem "![](images/93/936cea1e20ef7bbc1eb66acf9dd564c61476f5bb68ee05e553b00aaf3f970dc3.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible? \n\n- $x\\in [-4,0]$\n- $x\\in (-4,-2]$\n- $x\\in (-3,1)$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in [-4,0]$ and $x\\in (-4,-2].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/a4/a4499c2d4995c09b9ab6f8852cc2982f415f1650d65429c86df83800344106f4.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $x\\in (-3,1]$  because some lines will intersect the curve at more than one point.\n\n![](images/cb/cb0a462b3af10d15bb586f127baac45a74eecd5dc19848bda23664e75de968a4.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119783 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "055b3809-f9aa-5dcc-8c1a-b0eb71020d7e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a NxN Matrix Using Cofactor Expansion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e995287-b0ce-5d14-adf9-a8c9c41bfbc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321381} {:answer/id #uuid "2135e8a8-7aa1-5be7-a729-d18fff9337df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321397} {:answer/id #uuid "23cebe08-003e-58dd-b604-3b895669fb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321421} {:answer/id #uuid "30a88961-6b47-51d0-bc38-90b444ad5cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321521} {:answer/id #uuid "ec560ecf-e9c3-5994-aee2-a1534c401d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30a88961-6b47-51d0-bc38-90b444ad5cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321521} :answer-field/id #uuid "9029e3eb-d47c-517f-8e68-1e0ac69f0975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5505f107-cfef-57b0-b433-16953d2d1848" :question/math-academy-id "q-36045" :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$Z=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Z) & ={z}_{11}{C}_{11}+{z}_{12}{C}_{12}+{z}_{13}{C}_{13}+{z}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & ={C}_{11}-{C}_{13} \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & -3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ ∗ & 4 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & -1 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & ∗ & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & ∗ & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|-(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|+0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}|)-(0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|-0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|+0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|) \\\\  & =0+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|+0-(0-0+0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2((-3)\\cdot 5-4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-31) \\\\  & =-62\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119783 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "055b3809-f9aa-5dcc-8c1a-b0eb71020d7e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a NxN Matrix Using Cofactor Expansion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44a53c04-e529-5980-9871-488b48753234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321687} {:answer/id #uuid "705109d1-3af4-5ff3-acf7-5d3d0f081f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-218" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322023} {:answer/id #uuid "70f2e27b-b69d-5a8e-a251-9c44a0969327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-197" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322031} {:answer/id #uuid "825d1898-c1c5-515c-8d32-7d9391d79b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-255" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322174} {:answer/id #uuid "fd14dd4b-300e-50c6-82b4-70959e79fea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "825d1898-c1c5-515c-8d32-7d9391d79b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-255" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322174} :answer-field/id #uuid "9edb3717-adce-570c-8b92-ef6311946dc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f3bd215-2eb4-5f41-af3f-5119c1a64176" :question/math-academy-id "q-36055" :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={a}_{11}{C}_{11}+{a}_{12}{C}_{12}+{a}_{13}{C}_{13}+{a}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+5\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =-3{C}_{11}+5{C}_{14} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ ∗ & -1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ ∗ & 6 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|+5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & ∗ \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 & ∗ \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|+5(-1)^{1+4}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|-5|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3(0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|-3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}|) \\\\  & -5(0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}|-0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{aligned}|) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-3\\cdot (0+18)+(-2)\\cdot (0-12))-5\\cdot (3\\cdot (24-1)) \\\\  & =90-345 \\\\  & =-255\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf7f11d9-6aa8-5922-a5fa-89f7dba66644" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two That Require Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29305cc3-43d0-55fb-bee6-c116386d6840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|+\\frac{9}{t+1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321467} {:answer/id #uuid "5652458c-4116-513d-a118-cc95260347bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|-\\frac{1}{t+1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321829} {:answer/id #uuid "7dcd1d72-f52c-5d31-b71e-3c0f50c8c226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|+\\frac{9}{t+1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322137} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ec1376-99f7-53f3-ba53-5e7ce309e2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|-\\frac{2}{t+1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322474} {:answer/id #uuid "df894ff5-be11-5a29-b3d1-ae226d7755f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|-\\frac{9}{t+1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df894ff5-be11-5a29-b3d1-ae226d7755f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|-\\frac{9}{t+1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322896} :answer-field/id #uuid "90813ad7-313c-50bc-891f-789675573687" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "667a9a42-df15-5bd2-89df-f07d8b3e9276" :question/math-academy-id "q-3612" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{5{t}^{2}+20t+6}{{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}+t}\\,\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5{t}^{2}+20t+6}{{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}+t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\frac{5{t}^{2}+20t+6}{t(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{t}^{2}+20t+6}{t(t+1)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{t}+\\frac{B}{t+1}+\\frac{C}{(t+1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $t(t+1)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n5{t}^{2}+20t+6=A(t+1)^{2}+B(t)(t+1)+C(t).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(0)^{2}+20(0)+6 & =A(0+1)^{2}+B(0)(0+1)+C(0) \\\\ 6 & =A \\\\ A & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $t=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-1)^{2}+20(-1)+6 & =A(-1+1)^{2}+B(-1)(-1+1)+C(-1) \\\\ -9 & =-C \\\\ C & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $t=1$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)^{2}+20(1)+6 & =A(1+1)^{2}+B(1)(1+1)+C(1) \\\\ 31 & =4A+2B+C \\\\ 31 & =4(6)+2B+(9) \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{t}^{2}+20t+6}{t(t+1)^{2}}=\\frac{6}{t}-\\frac{1}{t+1}+\\frac{9}{(t+1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{t}^{2}+20t+6}{t(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{(6)}{t}+\\frac{(-1)}{t+1}+\\frac{(9)}{(t+1)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-\\int \\frac{1}{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t+9\\int \\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|-\\frac{9}{t+1}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "116c34e0-5cfd-5bb3-964d-9f2713592207" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function With an Irreducible Trinomial Quadratic Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a07e8c-0d67-55a0-881c-3c7ad3584a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321306} {:answer/id #uuid "143c3dc4-b275-59ab-82da-1687d8c3670b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321312} {:answer/id #uuid "408f6414-20da-5d9d-9573-95a8e2965353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321657} {:answer/id #uuid "791821d2-5503-575c-8113-5c6cf088de0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322100} {:answer/id #uuid "8cad3b73-177a-52f5-b52e-90d072312464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "143c3dc4-b275-59ab-82da-1687d8c3670b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321312} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb63bad1-66a8-55a7-a0fa-643ca9560f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96d5da43-5132-5ee1-8491-d36fb395f680" :question/math-academy-id "q-3615" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\nD=(-2)^{2}-4(1)(3)=-8<0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}+\\frac{C}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-3 & =(Ax+B)(x+1)+C({x}^{2}-2x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get  $A=\\frac{3}{2},$ $B=-\\frac{3}{2},$ and $C=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{3x-3}{2({x}^{2}-2x+3)}-\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration only at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3x-3}{2({x}^{2}-2x+3)}-\\frac{1}{2(x+1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}-2x+3.$ Then $(2x-2)\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-2x+3| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "116c34e0-5cfd-5bb3-964d-9f2713592207" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function With an Irreducible Trinomial Quadratic Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5af545d0-dcb5-5648-ab4f-0b94b13d63e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+\\frac{1}{2x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321864} {:answer/id #uuid "93d40251-d875-5f5d-aef0-72ef06d9ec10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+\\frac{2}{x-1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322322} {:answer/id #uuid "d60bd1aa-c2d7-5998-9acb-c89cddd5adb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+\\frac{2}{x-1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322817} {:answer/id #uuid "e6778e04-6c08-54d2-a3df-10a120183457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322951} {:answer/id #uuid "e726d17c-3f3d-5cec-800c-b9ec3bd91662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e726d17c-3f3d-5cec-800c-b9ec3bd91662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322958} :answer-field/id #uuid "883e337e-d198-5733-933e-966f03198bed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "acee9da4-09d4-567b-82f2-79a4b4042b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-3617" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a discriminant that is not a perfect square:\n\n$$\nD={4}^{2}-4(1)(-1)=20\n$$\n\nWe have that $\\sqrt{20}$ is not a rational number, which implies that the quadratic factor doesn't factor over the rational numbers.\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+9x-4=A({x}^{2}+4x-1)+(Bx+C)(x-1)\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=1$ and $C=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}=\\frac{2}{x-1}+\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x-1}+\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n2\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+4x-1.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68e983da-1f69-5be1-8034-9422a84ce6be" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor and Another Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32aefbe2-2512-5bba-8ebb-015d2a67ba32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+\\frac{9}{x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321534} {:answer/id #uuid "84d231e3-121c-5767-9d06-6b09a08b8dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-3)^{2}|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322197} {:answer/id #uuid "8a3b601b-7e73-5e53-ab46-77675db02195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+\\frac{9}{x-3}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322251} {:answer/id #uuid "99062e76-8cd0-51a3-8f37-420071f6e409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\frac{2}{x-3}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322362} {:answer/id #uuid "992437bc-efa9-57ea-8b6e-bb16320f6777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\frac{9}{x-3}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "992437bc-efa9-57ea-8b6e-bb16320f6777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\frac{9}{x-3}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322364} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aabc6f3-0acb-533f-9fdd-acb8d584d226" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f0631c5-dc78-5feb-807b-cc910eff2885" :question/math-academy-id "q-3619" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x-2)(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x-2)(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{x-3}+\\frac{C}{(x-3)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)(x-3)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=A(x-3)^{2}+B(x-3)(x-2)+C(x-2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3)^{2} & =A(3-3)^{2}+B(3-3)(3-2)+C(3-2) \\\\ 9 & =C \\\\ C & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$  we can eliminate  $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2} & =A(2-3)^{2}+B(2-3)(2-2)+C(2-2) \\\\ 4 & =A \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2} & =A(0-3)^{2}+B(0-3)(0-2)+C(0-2) \\\\ 0 & =9A+6B-2C \\\\ 0 & =9(4)+6B-2(9) \\\\ 0 & =18+6B \\\\ -6B & =18 \\\\ B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x-2)(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{3}{x-3}+\\frac{9}{(x-3)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x-2)(x-3)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{3}{x-3}+\\frac{9}{(x-3)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x+9\\int \\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\frac{9}{x-3}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b51e8ff1-247b-5efd-a684-8fca6c064e1e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Power of an Idempotent Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d15d9f3-732b-55fc-82a8-ea78dc52e1fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391960} {:answer/id #uuid "62095833-a3c7-56bd-8dae-b6e79ea4ea2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391961} {:answer/id #uuid "57d7afe0-ca6f-57d3-a2e3-906ad22f4aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391962} {:answer/id #uuid "37bb6378-9f91-5ead-9cda-c102778d5f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391963} {:answer/id #uuid "9580bf69-88fc-535e-8e59-7eee0bdecc7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62095833-a3c7-56bd-8dae-b6e79ea4ea2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391961} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e7b743d-c43a-510a-a794-32f1c29c8d88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2594c879-7fd1-5b85-9df5-7ae761ff92cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-36302" :question/problem "If $C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, then ${C}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${C}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${C}^{2}=C,$ so $C$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${C}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{3} & ={C}^{2}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{C}^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115229 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82e970fe-cff8-50e3-9b94-67ad97b03da7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Power of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{