[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391968} {:answer/id #uuid "3b685a74-d06e-5470-8105-b7c69e714300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -9 \\\\ -18 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391969} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2929bd-3255-5ff4-9d4b-394d060e9fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d2929bd-3255-5ff4-9d4b-394d060e9fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391970} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9087050-2a63-5da9-ab8a-c09ea4fea4df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c346e870-b148-514c-829b-61b66a0ee82d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36314" :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, then ${A}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, to find ${A}^{2},$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${A}^{4}={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "116c34e0-5cfd-5bb3-964d-9f2713592207" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function With an Irreducible Trinomial Quadratic Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "152b9b90-2369-58a8-81aa-4d8afdc8db6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321319} {:answer/id #uuid "4d157c35-4de2-5ea9-ac5a-0dc538c67393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321756} {:answer/id #uuid "58b14230-2948-5c88-9c68-1caa5b9a5b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)-10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321844} {:answer/id #uuid "5b160e16-6f6c-54d6-abb8-f7a49ab67f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321865} {:answer/id #uuid "bea1fd27-c40f-5dbb-a78f-1c5fa4e8ae12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b160e16-6f6c-54d6-abb8-f7a49ab67f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321865} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dcf6ee9-5e88-5b9b-aa22-7d6ea3f8eae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "731b8f94-fb6c-56e8-9abe-5ac794eb1b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-3638" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\nD=(-6)^{2}-4(2)(9)=-36<0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}+\\frac{C}{x-4}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n15-25x=(Ax+B)(x-4)+C(2{x}^{2}-6x+9).\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=10,B=-15$ and $C=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}=\\frac{10x-15}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}+\\frac{-5}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{10x-15}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}+\\frac{-5}{x-4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u=2{x}^{2}-6x+9.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(4x-6)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}-6x+9| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\frac{2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9))-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f3bfa4-c874-5502-b110-2b8c72d11d3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321187} {:answer/id #uuid "0676db20-db96-5e83-9dd9-af9bc2d62d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321198} {:answer/id #uuid "89cc5679-849e-548d-a951-2998d3ec013f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322248} {:answer/id #uuid "c04ceba5-0448-5d21-beab-cccea2bd0eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322641} {:answer/id #uuid "ea682fc8-18e7-53c1-b083-08279d59916f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89cc5679-849e-548d-a951-2998d3ec013f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322248} :answer-field/id #uuid "87a0d8da-f7a1-5f82-9cfc-3a0cc85e80a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f36893d-e053-5d18-beeb-2f0d972c9d98" :question/math-academy-id "q-365323" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${4}^{1-2x}\\le 3\\cdot {5}^{2-3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{1-2x} & \\le 3\\cdot {5}^{2-3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{1-2x}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {5}^{2-3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{1-2x}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{2-3x}) \\\\ (1-2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡4-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits of Piecewise Functions with a Gap in the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6489f85-c078-529a-bb89-256282a9a13d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402497} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee1227f-4f52-5de5-b34e-e560db110256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402498} {:answer/id #uuid "b65d528b-38e9-53d6-b9b7-00b8f1c5ac41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402499} {:answer/id #uuid "e70c9d48-daf4-5c24-8f93-c596a8791141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402500} {:answer/id #uuid "668245ac-4011-59ee-a836-e35d283d1eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b65d528b-38e9-53d6-b9b7-00b8f1c5ac41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402499} :answer-field/id #uuid "9766008d-bd13-5a70-b8fc-147a47588ba7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65911f9a-fa00-54b8-9673-9586456d002e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36696" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The right-sided limit ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nApproaching $x=0$ from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100977 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0995bfd4-20b1-5f12-9d1d-fcb180a6291e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19ed57d1-1d91-544a-bdae-7940b7113ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321348} {:answer/id #uuid "877e8444-5a27-500f-a477-baed7db698bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322223} {:answer/id #uuid "964d5dce-dd1f-5775-a66c-aba0c11d0274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322343} {:answer/id #uuid "98de86f1-ea9d-579c-b52c-2a809a3accce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322361} {:answer/id #uuid "e14ed661-5e3a-5437-bf1b-eb0a7535207c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e14ed661-5e3a-5437-bf1b-eb0a7535207c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322907} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf487efd-f750-56a9-8fe4-aff8fa623557" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df0978e3-40c7-5749-aacd-3714af936e64" :question/math-academy-id "q-36720" :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}6x+1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -4{log}_{2}⁡x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=6x+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}(6x+1) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow,  approaching from the right, we have $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-4{log}_{2}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}-4{log}_{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡{2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-4(-1){log}_{2}⁡2 \\\\  & =-4(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0331564f-2588-501b-b00b-4bfc21903eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321178} {:answer/id #uuid "26f8feb1-5ef3-5db8-83c3-0253bb71a162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321454} {:answer/id #uuid "30a31cb0-e610-5382-bc68-23ae87a10893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321520} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9a9190-a31e-5666-90db-d2259e210b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322279} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e36c4d-2ff0-5296-a228-00cad37c904e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26f8feb1-5ef3-5db8-83c3-0253bb71a162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321454} :answer-field/id #uuid "6db0b422-9f7f-5b42-ba98-b50b2a2c0d76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2777af2b-a5d2-5c98-9043-4dca19a2df59" :question/math-academy-id "q-36724" :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=3$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi )\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\sqrt[3]{3x-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3\\cdot 3-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are different, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124472 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22d016a6-ae62-56e2-94ca-cf268596c630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402518} {:answer/id #uuid "07a9ea11-bf6b-5bd3-bc0f-8446bc1bf4cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402519} {:answer/id #uuid "89edd0ab-89db-5aea-aa06-1774d0033938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402520} {:answer/id #uuid "bf173099-427d-522f-a04c-39870e96d661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402521} {:answer/id #uuid "5e473cc0-e97e-5768-bad0-fa0e92282062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89edd0ab-89db-5aea-aa06-1774d0033938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402520} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ab0fae8-fb4f-560c-b176-a3a930105d51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cbc83e7-bc89-5609-b94a-0fe1dfb1bde0" :question/math-academy-id "q-36905" :question/problem "![](images/91/91f073fa77aab423ee33902451e61ad9f033b61b2a875b66ff69708d4361e24f.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\nFrom the above, we see that in each of the four given graphs, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. Therefore, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, none of them correspond to an invertible function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb35fcff-d9f0-5ae8-999e-7e8bbc0b0919" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cardinality of a Finite Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0df71174-ba47-505b-bfd7-f8516b361d70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321253} {:answer/id #uuid "423b333b-ab94-596f-9eb6-20ace4aa035d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321672} {:answer/id #uuid "7c7ef370-eb04-5e07-a559-e09855d604e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322127} {:answer/id #uuid "93fa9430-ac63-5570-bbf0-5157f1c646d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322325} {:answer/id #uuid "b838e181-ede3-5fed-bdef-2abda2ebfbdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "423b333b-ab94-596f-9eb6-20ace4aa035d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321672} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4798abf-4c8f-5c72-89b7-5b2e39f93e13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d949443f-9b1e-55e7-9de3-ec4a5ebd3a6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36915" :question/problem "If $C={♣,♠,♡,♢,△,♣}$, then $|C|=$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $C$ is the number of elements in $C.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $C:$\n\n$$\n♣,\\,♠,\\,♡,\\,♢,\\,△\n$$\n\nNote that only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\nSince $C$ has $5$ elements, its cardinality is $|C|=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4caea11f-484f-50a4-a520-52df284329ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321753} {:answer/id #uuid "54e75cb9-e5b0-5ea8-9eaf-304e5de715df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321816} {:answer/id #uuid "888f7548-fc8c-5732-aae9-9e3aa70555ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322236} {:answer/id #uuid "c304d552-6f05-5067-a107-0c7b855a1c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322661} {:answer/id #uuid "f99f1c2f-bc18-5796-a3e2-28f9f9b5b60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "888f7548-fc8c-5732-aae9-9e3aa70555ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322236} :answer-field/id #uuid "9392c5de-5972-51d1-8356-6d7fe3ba1f53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e891cfb3-f978-5b44-b8bf-4d4f9f71b503" :question/math-academy-id "q-36941" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3cbdac583a4cdf1b5eb761892934516a0b4edd1487bd402ae0014859c4a0ad53.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (-2,0]$\n\nII. $x\\in (-2,2]$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-4,-2]\\cup [2,4]$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,0].$ Indeed, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/d7/d7c6e8d314483ad67e89ce5d4675b7af27fa08c88a4ed9d95eed9ad2a4da51cc.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-2,2]$ and $x\\in [-4,-2]\\cup [2,4]$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/ce/cee829f7e3d5f32b0acb54cb5c40406d8391d2a2967c68df0c1f5e1cae87d729.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "231604dc-fe8f-59be-90fe-37af7a1bb648" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has no Vertical Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f2dcf3-b7da-5207-a75c-12f5aab228de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321324} {:answer/id #uuid "2d333293-812e-550f-be94-d57de33d86b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321498} {:answer/id #uuid "3e848eef-3137-5776-aaca-65af02d8767e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321638} {:answer/id #uuid "40ee8b23-a651-5c65-b7f7-c17eaad7aa2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321664} {:answer/id #uuid "77dc533c-b6a2-5d5c-aab6-a0419fb093db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40ee8b23-a651-5c65-b7f7-c17eaad7aa2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321664} :answer-field/id #uuid "7758aae7-f886-5729-92ed-f1adeb80d088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07860f00-c712-5f63-bfe2-a24e46e0db46" :question/math-academy-id "q-37070" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-3x}{8x-6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{2}-3x}{8x-6} & =\\frac{x(4x-3)}{2(4x-3)}=\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e6ebc8eaec86276442b13fde934234fdaf9c4a457398bb1b792c6910350e44c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "109ac02d-edd3-5542-85a6-fe4488ed982c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={-1,0,1,2,4,6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321279} {:answer/id #uuid "146cebbe-47c4-5f92-bb21-db3162be426f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={1,3,5,7,9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321313} {:answer/id #uuid "23131580-17fd-59eb-a6fc-22211fca432b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={2,6,12,14,32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321414} {:answer/id #uuid "ee78ef16-f1f1-5ece-b981-30dd16ac8798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={0,1,2,4,6,8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323026} {:answer/id #uuid "f72afeec-f234-514d-b514-5e3bea13abb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={2,4,6,7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23131580-17fd-59eb-a6fc-22211fca432b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={2,6,12,14,32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321414} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ced3dc9-18a6-5c14-baa5-67b07af90bd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f34c2a60-9466-5441-87ed-4e11996631ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-37265" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={2n∣n\\in N}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $A.$ The elements of the set \n$A$ are all the natural even numbers.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA={2,4,6,8,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, a subset of $A$ is a set whose elements are also elements of the set $A.$\n\nIn our case, $B={2,6,12,14,32}$ is a subset of $A.$\n\nIn the rest of the options, the set $B$ has elements that are not in $A,$ meaning that it is not a subset of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1285179b-e40e-5454-b147-730f7625e9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321289} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad1106e-0b1d-59af-a477-858bb3ed6b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321735} {:answer/id #uuid "5390e444-9b80-53b6-815c-4d1943e938c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321804} {:answer/id #uuid "c08f3962-f686-54ff-b63a-cae429363ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322645} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0b5366-cd3b-5d93-bab1-d1080020ae04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ad1106e-0b1d-59af-a477-858bb3ed6b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321735} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e749b67-7850-53ec-94ef-c715ea07c273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c42f9a6a-c252-590f-89ec-76e86350914b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37266" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={3,6,9,12,15,18,21,…}?$\n\n- $B={3n:n\\in N,1\\le n\\le 4}$\n- $C={6n:n\\in N,1\\le n\\le 3}$\n- $D={2n:n\\in N,1\\le n\\le 4}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $B,C$ and $D.$\n\n- We find the elements of the set $B$ substituting the values $n\\in {1,2,3,4}$ into the formula $3n,$ and we get the following elements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=1 & \\,⇒\\,3(1)=3 \\\\ n=2 & \\,⇒\\,3(2)=6 \\\\ n=3 & \\,⇒\\,3(3)=9 \\\\ n=4 & \\,⇒\\,3(4)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have, $B={3,6,9,12}.$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $C$ can be found substituting the values $n\\in {1,2,3}$ into the formula $6n,$ and we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=1 & \\,⇒\\,6(1)=6 \\\\ n=2 & \\,⇒\\,6(2)=12 \\\\ n=3 & \\,⇒\\,6(3)=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C={6,12,18}.$\n\n- Finally, the elements of the set $D$ can be found substituting the values $n\\in {1,2,3,4}$ into the formula $2n,$ and we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=1 & \\,⇒\\,2(1)=2 \\\\ n=2 & \\,⇒\\,2(2)=4 \\\\ n=3 & \\,⇒\\,2(3)=6 \\\\ n=4 & \\,⇒\\,2(4)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have, $D={2,4,6,8}.$\n\nNow, a set is a subset of $A$ if all of its elements are also elements of $A.$ With that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- $B={3,6,9,12}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- $C={6,12,18}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- $D={2,4,6,8}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element $2\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f56bdd5-a607-5af2-b88c-b12454f5dbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392103} {:answer/id #uuid "f80ff926-3aa7-5c1c-b968-b56b246178c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392104} {:answer/id #uuid "544549f0-b759-5c27-aa3c-4223a9df60df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392105} {:answer/id #uuid "4fccaa0b-2b3d-58c3-881e-5c696c14c18c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392106} {:answer/id #uuid "8a29317f-e4cb-5886-99c5-3539d5b2e238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a29317f-e4cb-5886-99c5-3539d5b2e238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392107} :answer-field/id #uuid "04efa2b5-a248-5966-8fd4-c3f5d9dc1a1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14d10373-1baf-5303-9f9f-7dbe595ac72a" :question/math-academy-id "q-37488" :question/problem "Find the left-sided limit at $x=0$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x\\le 0 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124661 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32cbf366-20bd-5a52-a14f-83add698bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Rational Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "400f0091-175f-5587-84b8-9c92e0445d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321651} {:answer/id #uuid "463439b5-0cfc-525b-89ed-52c822437a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321701} {:answer/id #uuid "689d0a5e-3cbe-5251-93c7-9224aa737768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Does not exist." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321963} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbe2c23-cb67-5e33-ae6d-6470a9763727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323149} {:answer/id #uuid "feda0822-7e47-547f-b9e2-686fb2b48328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "463439b5-0cfc-525b-89ed-52c822437a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321701} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7b2c792-031f-57b9-a80b-617c4464ff0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be3f2ca8-5451-5c14-b7e4-a6be8ff17159" :question/math-academy-id "q-3762" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6{x}^{3}-9}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6{x}^{3}-9} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-2+\\frac{1}{x})}{(6-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d865bfb4-6ff5-577a-a992-c481af8842b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Singular 3x3 System of Equations with Two Pivots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d35eed70-a904-57c6-8b36-1d024048da1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392130} {:answer/id #uuid "324311ee-397f-5fcf-8380-cacd9ae6d35d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392131} {:answer/id #uuid "a325f15c-6e90-5bde-9158-27dc61018674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392132} {:answer/id #uuid "b3156e84-9b52-5f08-a0d8-db8b8472491c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392133} {:answer/id #uuid "02b183e3-094a-5114-879b-f7cae7db166d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3156e84-9b52-5f08-a0d8-db8b8472491c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392133} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c57e752-231d-5c0a-9ac8-edbce417ffee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bafb401-a99c-58a5-8622-a9ebf82083bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-37649" :question/problem "Consider the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{3}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124515 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd0537f8-b091-580a-974e-8e928d54939d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Under a Linear Transformation Given its Standard Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ef73df9-84ec-5f34-a71b-fe726195bfd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321383} {:answer/id #uuid "3efa9ca2-24ec-5607-8469-50498346ff15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321642} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9025d4-18ab-5385-94bd-26181314bc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322992} {:answer/id #uuid "eedbea08-593d-5c3a-a72c-6b6885d23514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323029} {:answer/id #uuid "fa65dcba-2e33-5614-8e1f-2c508af52fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3efa9ca2-24ec-5607-8469-50498346ff15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321642} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba434382-4384-5494-9593-15482668b874" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12f3bb3a-f83c-5bc7-89c6-1af5031ec24d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37881" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $T$ and the vector $v$ given below. If $T$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T,$ find $T(v).$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that $\\,T(v)=T\\cdot v,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =T\\cdot v \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91fbca89-c577-5e53-936f-3be4371d6739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322305} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d2dab8-cede-584b-9e20-5115b3d14dfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322441} {:answer/id #uuid "e53eb49d-9767-5e28-a2f3-ab6cd8a7a0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322942} {:answer/id #uuid "ed71d143-9343-52d1-af9f-9b40f8a55525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323019} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9ee2db-b77f-5cab-9284-b745cebd2507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4d2dab8-cede-584b-9e20-5115b3d14dfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322441} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bfbccbb-cbf5-52c1-a3ca-3a74ad96eac6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb646a61-3e18-55e1-850b-c5318af9cd57" :question/math-academy-id "q-37900" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ and $f(-1)=-3,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=f(-1)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8126b5-2054-5d88-865f-be6cbe674d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321368} {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} {:answer/id #uuid "51eee8c3-23ea-5613-a452-4c2182222cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321794} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba4b8d7-052a-5cf2-86db-50636fe22d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321871} {:answer/id #uuid "c2131ada-4ea2-5f29-85f1-aa42dc442a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} :answer-field/id #uuid "617b107f-dc16-5899-aab5-ab0f5850cc8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9112180a-ac6b-5472-ba8b-819d346ead1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37915" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c36dbce847c719accc77fa15f33dfb9a87cfae669691afe30f7693663076d2e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/52fe94bb303a001e89f9db221e9730d4ca8c5c284217aa20fb6084ee041dec66.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{10}{16}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{20}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402588 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03061225-179d-5595-8b56-b7d6f2d13215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{3}$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402590} {:answer/id #uuid "a674a2bd-163e-5ad8-b61d-3081527c6933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402591} {:answer/id #uuid "7282a493-7961-561e-9ff5-6f77e37d9805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $3$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402592} {:answer/id #uuid "03597655-1b81-562c-95ee-e8102a15928e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402593} {:answer/id #uuid "30627c9d-0160-5ed5-9e1b-4c1eae7cdbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $3$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7282a493-7961-561e-9ff5-6f77e37d9805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $3$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402592} :answer-field/id #uuid "86c45944-9a15-5a38-bc56-297085d279dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8007798-26fd-5f48-9d8f-a07b9c92e47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38020" :question/problem "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of $\\,{R}^{2}.$ If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=3j,$ then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\,i\\,$ and  $\\,j.$\n\n![](images/f2/f2438da7f6f0a2ab7e6653cad7776ef7d01faba55d22a480a98c152e81f0d78a.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=3j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](images/86/8674e7345c903bf193e09cc9ae1702383cad10ba06c5dc3b6d2713ea30eb843e.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch with a scale factor $3$ in the $y$-direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "979859e4-b60c-548d-8da6-b180400e1b07" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205ccffc-01c3-5074-bcb8-cc4b974d5c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324240} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb5b773-84ff-5a8d-8b87-3631203e61e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324319} {:answer/id #uuid "822fd678-004a-5da8-9c3e-6a6e50c794ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325066} {:answer/id #uuid "c67d2e1d-8417-5a78-a35c-8205ba48cf31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325685} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ea0889-e7f0-58ed-8cf7-e8e35863a9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c67d2e1d-8417-5a78-a35c-8205ba48cf31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325685} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4aa40ce-8299-5985-952f-3e95c613ab8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93db7f8f-7ceb-59cb-bb66-3026f7154ffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-38176" :question/problem "Find the value of $|\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =6|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|+7|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6(-2-0)-0+7(0-2) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-2)+7\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-12-14 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "979859e4-b60c-548d-8da6-b180400e1b07" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11f2d595-4c96-599f-b04a-ef1a4facbf7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324134} {:answer/id #uuid "35476165-d1db-5ee5-b183-b3201978c25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324408} {:answer/id #uuid "36554c2e-a686-5080-983a-7fd2be339db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324414} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc9de58-8130-508b-ad5a-dc48cfe4914d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324751} {:answer/id #uuid "a45499fc-34c3-5b71-b300-396e74e38e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11f2d595-4c96-599f-b04a-ef1a4facbf7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324134} :answer-field/id #uuid "17302b73-c3c5-5ca7-9ed6-b18a09cd2b48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "687c1640-263b-54f0-8cd7-1fc76a39da2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38180" :question/problem "Find the value of $|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0+1(0-6)+3(-2-0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-6)+3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-6-6 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0e0a56b-dd12-5f9a-a54c-f687e7197f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402611} {:answer/id #uuid "c948f61c-0763-5a74-b23e-25debfd86016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402612} {:answer/id #uuid "ea7451af-09f5-51f1-a1a1-522af10914c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402613} {:answer/id #uuid "957a2ea8-95c2-5ef9-84c2-69527df0f48d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402614} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b56d91-8a6f-561f-b452-5b48fa80a33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1b56d91-8a6f-561f-b452-5b48fa80a33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402615} :answer-field/id #uuid "22d26b64-8f4f-5847-bc42-645a93a0286d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5eb8a6b-37c6-54e6-b340-18101dbf3f8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38187" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nI. $N$ is **not** a singular matrix\nII. $N$ is a singular matrix\nIII. $det(N)=-1$\n\n$$\nN=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} & 3 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 4 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 5 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} & 3 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 4 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 5 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}|\\begin{aligned}4 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 5 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}|+(-\\frac{1}{7})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} & 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(-\\frac{4}{7}+\\frac{5}{7})-3(-\\frac{1}{49}+\\frac{1}{49})-\\frac{1}{7}(\\frac{5}{7}-\\frac{4}{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}-3\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{49}-\\frac{1}{49} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "874fff2e-ad05-545d-b9da-54afafb38522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} = kD, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127310} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbdf968-a3aa-5c7c-bee0-f3e5cdef2d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} =-kt, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127311} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4bda65-3235-5e5f-bca3-363e844b6c69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} = kt, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127312} {:answer/id #uuid "1d0e73de-fef3-542f-948e-ab7d50987655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} =-\\frac{k}{t^{2}}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127313} {:answer/id #uuid "77b6d1c9-13bd-55b9-bf57-aaf485a87dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} = kt^{2}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127314}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce1a994-8031-5aae-8482-b5c5180c0212" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127309} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c5a7d28-429d-5869-a68d-4d92f36195cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402624} {:answer/id #uuid "c87a8f69-4b14-5096-8f83-ef9eed495b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402625} {:answer/id #uuid "ed230839-dcea-57e5-a62d-204f0b996646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402626} {:answer/id #uuid "7b35292d-237a-56fe-bfb7-1ff65aaee600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=kD,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402627} {:answer/id #uuid "78931a87-f659-54d0-8ce9-c7e8a7e38fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c87a8f69-4b14-5096-8f83-ef9eed495b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402625} :answer-field/id #uuid "a733e896-0f87-5db4-b6c7-ab7a478c7bf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f8683314-e1cb-5e62-aca4-60ca1e1c5bf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-38313" :question/problem "A skater is skating straight down a ramp. The displacement $D$ of the skater increases at a rate that's proportional to the square of the time $t,$ in seconds.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of displacement $D$ at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time since he started skating?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*A skater is skating straight down a ramp. The **displacement** $D$ of the skater **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t,$ in seconds. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *displacement* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *square of the time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}∝{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the displacement *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171961 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e195636-f824-59a4-abdf-d31228b4187a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{2})\\cup (0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324224} {:answer/id #uuid "4fa3d88a-2b61-59ff-b38c-9e117f5d0322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,0)\\cup (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324623} {:answer/id #uuid "66c05659-8960-56d9-bb8c-418ee8683644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324830} {:answer/id #uuid "7b285831-24e0-55ac-94b7-4dd8bf45c4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325006} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b3664a-7876-58dc-9d4d-2263cc2053bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fa3d88a-2b61-59ff-b38c-9e117f5d0322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,0)\\cup (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324623} :answer-field/id #uuid "36cefb10-087b-5e28-ba4a-6457c4bbeb43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59bb1ad4-d174-5fc9-b5c8-63da66cdfefc" :question/math-academy-id "q-38509" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-6}{x}>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-6}{x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(x+2)(2x-3)}{x}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,\\frac{3}{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-2,0)$ and $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-2,0)\\cup (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124543 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8725caed-481c-5fbe-a0c4-cffdcb308881" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Given With Respect to the Standard Basis Under a Linear Map"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "343d274b-7d30-577d-b930-e279eebc52cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392207} {:answer/id #uuid "6b758ebd-0068-500f-84f3-3efb15ccb301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392208} {:answer/id #uuid "5267bbc0-ee20-57da-b708-6ecd9aa4d8c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392209} {:answer/id #uuid "b382431d-4827-57f1-987f-04d858e33b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392210} {:answer/id #uuid "f119f9a5-4e43-576d-bccc-20901c834a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b758ebd-0068-500f-84f3-3efb15ccb301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392208} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bfcfd97-d86f-5038-b51e-12f8313c73ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b03e0038-023a-5069-84d1-998d6d535a68" :question/math-academy-id "q-38540" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ (shown above) and a basis $C$ of ${R}^{3}.$\nThe matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to these bases, and the vector $x\\in {R}^{2},$ are also given. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $[x]_{B},$ the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B.$ So, let\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives us the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now row-reduce the corresponding augmented matrix using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "374ae646-8b4e-5b0e-bd7e-4acb5d1c0ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324420} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1612fc-e261-50ce-9313-fe26f50738e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325026} {:answer/id #uuid "c86a33ca-8732-5643-a35e-d2538c0e58e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325709} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e6cb1a-5418-5746-8b18-030e1c8789a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325813} {:answer/id #uuid "f0206e22-2c8b-5150-92b8-e52b0d52a598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "374ae646-8b4e-5b0e-bd7e-4acb5d1c0ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324420} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b0c2133-1c76-5099-a8ab-82d13698a8d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa66e43c-92cc-5831-9942-3be250ef9ed8" :question/math-academy-id "q-38546" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fdb835ed2998fae4d8afc3fd5e593bac6c72b1ee45c8b4fe01bdc4348322d1e.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nI. The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n\nII. The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n\nIII. The function is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a78286a-924a-5b53-b1ff-20b6e774f358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324064} {:answer/id #uuid "38c11f6c-ec31-5baf-88b1-d11c59ff1a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324432} {:answer/id #uuid "4726d096-c73d-5a7c-a29b-ed3c56e58ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324547} {:answer/id #uuid "563239d5-e805-5360-a191-da957f460fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324689} {:answer/id #uuid "f3aeaf56-06ad-5d25-bdf7-6801ed375e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3aeaf56-06ad-5d25-bdf7-6801ed375e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326117} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba7f8325-4242-5de7-ae67-9b6277a9588d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f98bb945-e8b3-5349-97c2-0b4b023243ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-38547" :question/problem "![](images/18/188e0169738a08b3d4580b4f5d3a3ab1aedef87a9d1b83f07c1b10686a38c321.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true in relation to the function $y=g(x)$ above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is continuous at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x)=0\\ne -2=g(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x)=-2=g(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09148cf6-e660-5422-9fa7-a9f4738defd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402651} {:answer/id #uuid "3340c634-8f83-5193-ae75-2b37a7b6c946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402652} {:answer/id #uuid "f4481476-873e-5b4e-a202-210e8449c587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402653} {:answer/id #uuid "63b1b59c-8954-5973-ae03-cc16a86d080d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402654} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f0241d-3539-5ced-b138-fdde689dc4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3340c634-8f83-5193-ae75-2b37a7b6c946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402652} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f13134e-6c13-54bc-aaf2-8a968a13df74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4ced118-ede6-56c5-a59a-002b28515d4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38580" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea87f040e4249a04c21dd6c8466ffd194d60753056c29f00d8956e182db61ff3.png)\n\nThe graph of the polynomial $y=F(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The degree of $F(x)$ is odd.\nII. The degree of $F(x)$ is even.\nIII. The coefficient of the leading term is positive." :question/worked-solution "The end behavior of $F(x)$ is\n\n- $F(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ and\n\n- $F(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $F(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $F(x)$ is positive.\n\nSo II is false, and I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129119 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "117b0f2a-a546-5344-bfb8-3e0c41afbbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324131} {:answer/id #uuid "395db5bb-f296-567c-91ea-db87d69ea219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324435} {:answer/id #uuid "41eb7615-c4fd-5775-8878-99d3a9cb550f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k+V,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324504} {:answer/id #uuid "b71b1c89-8e55-546e-8485-90db0a36d0ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-V" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325544} {:answer/id #uuid "f047554c-a8e1-5e18-881a-83ed20299fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-tV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "117b0f2a-a546-5344-bfb8-3e0c41afbbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324131} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdf79fb1-73e0-5037-8b11-7d7606258ed9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eafaeb15-26b6-5234-a17e-a3acc08c3e0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-38593" :question/problem "The number of weekly sales of a particular book decreases with time. The number of units sold at any given moment decreases at a rate that's proportional to the number of units sold at that moment. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of units sold $V(t)$ at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time since the book first went on sale?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of units sold** at any given moment **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to **the number of units sold** at that moment.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of units sold* of a particular book varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of units sold.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝V.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of units sold *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "520ce037-6d65-5a87-816c-38b9a9d22f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324642} {:answer/id #uuid "b137372b-d54b-51cb-8c70-5c8eaabe8e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325482} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ee0489-97b3-566f-8f0e-c62d506da27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325704} {:answer/id #uuid "cb11e15c-7b44-5232-8e56-3e85080ab86c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325731} {:answer/id #uuid "e61483f3-87ce-5ef0-b035-b6a704d798b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b137372b-d54b-51cb-8c70-5c8eaabe8e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325482} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceb549ca-f772-54ab-ac92-58fb214b0833" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f40578d-2e88-5fde-bb73-48381fd621fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-38617" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb4959f437e11b767be136a2a024260d45ed29805cd57b1832d74d7cc2da11c1.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $y=P(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\nII. The degree of $P(x)$ is odd.\n\nIII. The coefficient of the leading term is negative." :question/worked-solution "The end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7cf112-ab08-5e7a-855d-91bb69ec7f76" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polynomial Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fbb6282-911b-5b66-b192-305d440d6e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x^{6} + x^{4} - 5x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127562} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a044cc-7963-5872-b7e6-6d9a2f224fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-7x^{8} + 6x^{7} - 4x^{3} + 2x^{2} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127563} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb2454f-bf3e-5aa4-afce-9896ef787f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-5x^{5} + 3x^{2} + x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127564} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5b9971-58f5-5d6d-9f95-7c155519b2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-x^{3} + 2x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127565} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0c079c-6769-57c1-b736-a89c4eef2463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{3} - x^{2} + x - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127566}] :answer-field/id #uuid "db6524ac-b4e5-521e-a137-1133e610aa7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127561} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb2ff6c4-35ee-5c86-88b6-f2b778bb9b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{3}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402657} {:answer/id #uuid "0cf46694-f517-57c1-b656-f30bd243fe5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-7{x}^{8}+6{x}^{7}-4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402658} {:answer/id #uuid "0cabba40-fb5e-5284-8447-f1cf64e6820e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{6}+{x}^{4}-5x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402659} {:answer/id #uuid "e52d814a-f821-5e6e-934c-6f5a0f7ba906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402660} {:answer/id #uuid "343e1b78-b8cf-5288-841e-bb541f7dedd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}+x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb2ff6c4-35ee-5c86-88b6-f2b778bb9b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{3}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402657} :answer-field/id #uuid "702788a0-aadc-5877-8e1c-f586a9038e3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e1b36cf-56ef-56e7-80a1-3a4e3d34ad42" :question/math-academy-id "q-38624" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d873bd2af8e397d6c540267340b6105f413928c4279b939110aa4bb5b00d8948.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is negative, which means that $P(0)<0.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, only $P(x)=-{x}^{3}+2x-1$ satisties all these conditions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7cf112-ab08-5e7a-855d-91bb69ec7f76" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polynomial Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ec8bba-61a9-5e53-9f83-ae6306a35d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{x}^{6}+\\sqrt{7}{x}^{3}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324133} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3e9e3c-8f63-5b55-a2b5-d6ae2f7cc85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324219} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6cc728-1b58-586c-b553-5f4c6de4cade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3{x}^{3}+8x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324575} {:answer/id #uuid "858b3ba7-0126-52d6-a92c-623111bcf464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{x}^{8}+4{x}^{2}+6x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325099} {:answer/id #uuid "d96579c4-211b-512e-819d-f2e5755afaf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6{x}^{5}+4{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d3e9e3c-8f63-5b55-a2b5-d6ae2f7cc85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324219} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c01c0f4-68e1-5f41-ac69-b50a572974f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c04700c1-06e0-50a6-8674-83b9d2f36915" :question/math-academy-id "q-38626" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb467eed4327c7feaa4cb0aad4495032fe52518891bb6d33227352c953ce4d35.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is positive, which means that $P(0)>0.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only one that satisfies all three criteria is $y=-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-x+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f76b4e-c2e3-5210-b8b9-db6bc53bafc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324510} {:answer/id #uuid "868d64bf-05a4-5a8a-bb2f-f215d1a3fb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325114} {:answer/id #uuid "aefcef1a-2486-5ad4-889b-d4796ad4702f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325458} {:answer/id #uuid "dfae7751-a48a-56fb-8fe9-dabe433a0205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325924} {:answer/id #uuid "fd71fe36-ab9d-5173-8340-e63507c5a1eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "868d64bf-05a4-5a8a-bb2f-f215d1a3fb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325114} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1d289fa-530e-56e8-81da-88ee4282c3b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbe22a70-053f-5354-8d7b-dc32dacb2db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-38770" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd19717bd41b787346a96394ce3bc4e38fe690340bb73bd3d11139d41ff977e2.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $x=2$ is a simple root.\n\nII. $x=-1$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n\nIII. $p(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=2$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=-1$  has an even multiplicity because at $x=-1$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ moves right in the positive direction towards $+\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves up in the positive direction towards $+\\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d88d6dd3-9da2-5bad-a373-7c62c3004b8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Quadratic “Or Equal To” Inequalities That Require Rearrangement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ed93f7f-faff-50dd-933d-c52ddd21b087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [0,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324344} {:answer/id #uuid "883f9638-fa98-5475-8b17-a6d88365a5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-4,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325131} {:answer/id #uuid "a656a7dd-92ac-5629-9919-f7b63b00baa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325387} {:answer/id #uuid "bd61994f-8110-5380-b33b-490999314a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325598} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe18b18-6dc1-5587-b41b-ce04d020783a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-4,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ed93f7f-faff-50dd-933d-c52ddd21b087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [0,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324344} :answer-field/id #uuid "69a718a0-ecd8-5ccc-add0-c55d3b2959c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4af160ec-0053-5463-9578-616321a05efa" :question/math-academy-id "q-3881" :question/problem "Find the solution of $-{x}^{2}\\ge -4x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2} & \\ge -4x \\\\ -{x}^{2}+4x & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+4x$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ -x(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=0$ and $x=4,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/d2/d234c29de2a39c664d6a03acf1d8cc43782914a3c82045cc103353ebaf67b897.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+4x\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nwe look for the part of the parabola that is above the $x$-axis or touches this axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $0\\le x\\le 4.$ \n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in [0,4].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129094 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eac988ba-ea97-52c7-90a7-d451ee7f49e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Rational Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2ecaae7-3c35-55c3-949a-8bc3ad796eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325506} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc63a98-85e5-5224-9b6a-a9c5d63399a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325592} {:answer/id #uuid "c44ce678-da9d-5026-bdf2-d1c31648eeec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325659} {:answer/id #uuid "cc959c14-7321-5ed8-8a0d-e560f10b3ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325745} {:answer/id #uuid "fb645e78-49d9-5243-b606-2ce3f8529efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,7)\\cup (7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2ecaae7-3c35-55c3-949a-8bc3ad796eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325506} :answer-field/id #uuid "aebe9ce5-b166-55c6-9070-84eeedf233a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90ecbfdb-7e05-5892-b0cb-107f95150e27" :question/math-academy-id "q-38831" :question/problem "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-{x}^{2}}{7x}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-{x}^{2}}{7x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(1-x)}{7x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-x}{7},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $x\\ne 0.$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded point $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,0),$ and $(0,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129302 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "503a2d0a-ba6c-5529-9f26-b83c769a26a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b52373-27a9-501e-8947-0c1f244a213e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324149} {:answer/id #uuid "5bec4237-30c8-54eb-a9f5-13ccaf205551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324732} {:answer/id #uuid "85ed53c0-2287-5169-b4e2-c59609989228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325105} {:answer/id #uuid "d964678d-9ceb-5d76-929a-65b4dd6a6052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325861} {:answer/id #uuid "df8ff16b-fe1f-5c14-b271-df5bace274b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df8ff16b-fe1f-5c14-b271-df5bace274b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325923} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9cd61a3-c73b-5511-815b-f6d529fa4ad2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45b870b0-b774-5b07-bef2-72f771e77f18" :question/math-academy-id "q-38999" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $y=\\frac{2}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83fad21d-6b36-5198-a13f-362d34342475" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Complex Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54c5bfcf-f18d-59e1-a9c7-3d228f60eaf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4-\\text{i} & 3+12\\text{i} \\\\ 2+4\\text{i} & 4+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392261} {:answer/id #uuid "cf089755-1d10-500b-a0a2-551f3bc3019b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4-\\text{i} & 3+12\\text{i} \\\\ 2+4\\text{i} & 4+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392262} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e63d90-d955-5848-a2e2-a18e3e4f0166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4+\\text{i} & -3-12\\text{i} \\\\ -2-4\\text{i} & -4-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392263} {:answer/id #uuid "a9746754-47aa-55a4-a6fd-ff02ba352d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4-\\text{i} & 3+12\\text{i} \\\\ 4+2\\text{i} & 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392264} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b99d12-86e4-57b5-bce0-9cfb8912ad79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 12\\text{i} \\\\ 2+4\\text{i} & 4+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf089755-1d10-500b-a0a2-551f3bc3019b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4-\\text{i} & 3+12\\text{i} \\\\ 2+4\\text{i} & 4+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392262} :answer-field/id #uuid "7064abf3-62e0-5e1b-afc9-1e54dab5330e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "469cfb30-4bd8-5f24-a8af-f1faf991da16" :question/math-academy-id "q-39021" :question/problem "If $\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5-\\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i} & -3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1-2\\text{i} & 1+2\\text{i} \\\\ -1 & 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$, then $AB=$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the matrices using the usual method, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5-\\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i} & -3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1-2\\text{i} & 1+2\\text{i} \\\\ -1 & 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1)(1-2\\text{i})+(5-\\text{i})(-1) & (1)(1+2\\text{i})+(5-\\text{i})(2\\text{i}) \\\\ (\\text{i})(1-2\\text{i})+(-3\\text{i})(-1) & (\\text{i})(1+2\\text{i})+(-3\\text{i})(2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1-2\\text{i})+(-5+\\text{i}) & (1+2\\text{i})+(2+10\\text{i}) \\\\ (2+\\text{i})+(3\\text{i}) & (-2+\\text{i})+(6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-\\text{i} & 3+12\\text{i} \\\\ 2+4\\text{i} & 4+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "135f42a4-d1ee-5f2c-af3a-0c8aa0176bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324143} {:answer/id #uuid "268df909-c8fb-563f-af2f-c08134584f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324283} {:answer/id #uuid "69e770f2-5fac-5581-b92a-b1b948231e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324857} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3c6363-b72d-5a0d-9eec-918fa9888a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325152} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9d3f67-6d9d-5067-8456-4fe529f4d545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69e770f2-5fac-5581-b92a-b1b948231e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324857} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f2953d1-fb0d-5e3e-8ec9-21bd1672c52d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f572c08e-edf8-5f9d-9827-c106bc98ece4" :question/math-academy-id "q-39033" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}-15x-25$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-25$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-25}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 25,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{25}{3}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ are $-1$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $3$ | $5$ | $-15$ | $-25$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $25$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-15$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})({x}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})(x+\\sqrt{5})(x-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{5}{3},-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{3}-\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d1d0905-b59d-5d4a-909c-cea1b665a291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402677} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fa7f39-114c-5150-972f-6a6f4f47133a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402678} {:answer/id #uuid "77d63650-bd84-5a65-ad3f-110fff12b852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402679} {:answer/id #uuid "a04b8362-fdb8-5b5f-84e2-c7356b9ecb6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402680} {:answer/id #uuid "6d67d68d-f930-5fbf-a8ad-1dfef015cd8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d1d0905-b59d-5d4a-909c-cea1b665a291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402677} :answer-field/id #uuid "570ae7f1-bab1-51b3-a682-ac5497a6d1ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "494506ed-3be9-5fa5-a304-e992bf8e5303" :question/math-academy-id "q-3946" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{2}-5x+16).$" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{2}-5x+16) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d88d6dd3-9da2-5bad-a373-7c62c3004b8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Quadratic “Or Equal To” Inequalities That Require Rearrangement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3537b38b-95ad-573f-bf1a-973a7f77c091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [0,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324406} {:answer/id #uuid "4160aec5-ae32-5d89-a3e6-59cb96379497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-4,\\frac{3}{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324500} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc6b821-6ead-5b81-b729-abdea0b7b47c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-1,\\frac{8}{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324589} {:answer/id #uuid "7b219723-295b-55da-8581-160cf8ed1375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-\\frac{1}{2},2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325005} {:answer/id #uuid "92ca5c4d-d8c2-54e5-b1ce-74fc0dbef7d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{11}{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4160aec5-ae32-5d89-a3e6-59cb96379497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-4,\\frac{3}{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324500} :answer-field/id #uuid "05dd1be7-dd1d-59c6-9395-c380cf562fdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9eaa518b-95b3-5d3f-9f89-7edbce2847db" :question/math-academy-id "q-3951" :question/problem "Find the solution of $2{x}^{2}-12\\le -5x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-12 & \\le -5x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+5x-12 & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}+5x-12$ lies below the $x$-axis or touches this axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+5x-12 & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+8x)+(-3x-12) & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+4)-3(x+4) & =0 \\\\ (2x-3)(x+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-4$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/ba/ba6c384800a5b42f2033527997328aa9f9d792b33b3fabecfe9b3a93789f46c4.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving\n \n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+5x-12\\le 0,\n$$\n\n we look for the part of the parabola that is below the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\n Based on the graph, the solution is $-4\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in [-4,\\frac{3}{2}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184335 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "530fc010-b15b-5877-8ed6-7fd2f8feb37c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Strict Quadratic Inequalities That Require Rearrangement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a3420d-46e5-5ca9-a685-0dbf5933828d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<0$ or $x>2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323996} {:answer/id #uuid "03c191d5-78e1-515b-b8d0-600e0c0382ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}<x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324011} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa9a870-78b8-5d01-a223-b95c5bb6deb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324196} {:answer/id #uuid "4fc7782a-b5d7-59b6-86d3-c0e69bd70297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-\\frac{1}{2}$ or $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324624} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd392c1-3348-5d04-af45-3831eb0e5ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}<x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aa9a870-78b8-5d01-a223-b95c5bb6deb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324196} :answer-field/id #uuid "a81897ee-57bb-5430-9cef-316566dd41d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1459c427-2f65-5af9-93bd-226984ba8bd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-3952" :question/problem "Find the solution of $-6{x}^{2}>-3x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{x}^{2} & >-3x \\\\ -6{x}^{2}+3x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=-6{x}^{2}+3x$ lies above the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ -3x(2x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/3d/3dff64c74abd4981c69686cbbf7d79fdd49c7a7fde3bb266b2b6e11c441bcf20.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n-6{x}^{2}+3x>0,\n$$\n\nwe look for the part of the parabola that is above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $0<x<\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24eebd01-d696-5583-866b-bc7ee0975ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $42$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324275} {:answer/id #uuid "5efa3e05-6fbd-561f-aad6-9039ff65a874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $39$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324765} {:answer/id #uuid "869369c2-c60a-5bfe-9e90-d7fc384dd4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $38$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325115} {:answer/id #uuid "e425578a-3944-5da8-bbf6-a67efada93ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $41$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325979} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c67aa3-aae6-50c4-9e30-cdb98ed82129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $40$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7c67aa3-aae6-50c4-9e30-cdb98ed82129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $40$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326012} :answer-field/id #uuid "1793d83a-a4d4-5328-b990-9f3e282f524a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5d1d92f-ad9b-5aee-bc72-594097ea04d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39648" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.001$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n64,\\,48,\\,36,\\,27,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=64$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{48}{64}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.001.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.001 \\\\ 64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <0.001 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.001}{64} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1≈39.386\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $40$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.001.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da68ea8-9055-54c3-9b30-270ae7a92ad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $13$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324221} {:answer/id #uuid "264d9efe-cf44-5392-934d-fa3a6cf67b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $14$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324281} {:answer/id #uuid "3e24d98b-b352-5855-b1dd-8137a0b991b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $10$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324474} {:answer/id #uuid "5367e798-cbb7-5217-b02f-1aa82697689b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324654} {:answer/id #uuid "93b68577-defa-5226-871d-34b1837def39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $11$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5367e798-cbb7-5217-b02f-1aa82697689b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324654} :answer-field/id #uuid "891f2714-aa56-5355-906f-1c3aa1b74936" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "904d2ffd-5671-50ed-98c6-a20c95b996b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-39655" :question/problem "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $5$, and the $6$th term is $40.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$2\\,020?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=5$ and ${a}_{6}=40$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{6-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{40}{5} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =8 \\\\ r & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{3}=5$ and  $r=2$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {2}^{3-1} \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 5 & =4{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {2}^{n-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,020.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {2}^{n-3} & >2\\,020 \\\\ {2}^{n-3} & >404 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{2}^{n-3} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(404) \\\\ (n-3)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(404) \\\\ n-3 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}+3≈11.658\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,020.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ad95b4e-e367-5e26-800c-1a87a537ca83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Trigonometric Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "433bf40e-b58f-5f73-acd3-043724f4f0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324514} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d08469-ad7b-5338-83f8-aa4eda61a77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325534} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4f7279-6b09-5d5a-b7dd-914bf38083f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325571} {:answer/id #uuid "eab55c63-7bc8-5884-94c9-5575e81cf09c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326040} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a36cbf-0b55-59cd-a76e-8d0bcaae5e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "433bf40e-b58f-5f73-acd3-043724f4f0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324514} :answer-field/id #uuid "c279fba3-e32a-5884-932a-62cb2020e6bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bdf79360-c3a2-5f70-b20e-0e9fca989978" :question/math-academy-id "q-39765" :question/problem "Find the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1})$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "The function is a composition of two functions, a rational function, and a sine function.\n\nThe sine is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the rational function.\n\nLikewise, the rational function $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1}$ is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(x-1)(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded points $x=-1$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain our original function consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1),$ $(-1,1),$ and $(1,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ad95b4e-e367-5e26-800c-1a87a537ca83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Trigonometric Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00cc317f-9860-521f-8e35-82b0b96775d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323979} {:answer/id #uuid "1336a17d-6837-5de5-bf71-47722d0cc700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324141} {:answer/id #uuid "20176902-9b53-52f9-8032-b69446a5fc1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324236} {:answer/id #uuid "a203df96-a113-582e-930a-799f5e42f2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325346} {:answer/id #uuid "d6617b71-5ede-536c-a0f7-693baf883f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20176902-9b53-52f9-8032-b69446a5fc1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324236} :answer-field/id #uuid "0394152c-8a6f-59be-83ce-2e7929fa5c84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ae959f9-6d60-5b89-84b8-0851a7c0c42d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39769" :question/problem "Find the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "The function is a composition of two functions, a radical function, and a cosine function.\n\nThe cosine is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the radical function.\n\nLikewise, the radical function $\\sqrt{2x+1}$ is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,2x\\ge -1\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is $[-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )$, and the function is continuous on this interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce1c8713-2cf3-5d3e-be72-a3135dfaed78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1,\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402690} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5c07fd-aef8-50a6-bb7a-0214e75d1fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=1,\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402691} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d397dd-0706-56c3-a545-4f1e11b5d1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402692} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3acbb2-152b-5386-a5b3-d4d5f9778946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402693} {:answer/id #uuid "466aa8d5-5559-5bc5-8a5d-ddd22b7bb453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9d397dd-0706-56c3-a545-4f1e11b5d1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402692} :answer-field/id #uuid "aba4d313-1d11-58c4-bb5b-3d3a12220a0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca4d349e-c1ee-5a19-a1e0-b0c9e7e02dd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-39793" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x}{(x+3)(x+1)(x-1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x+1)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the three solutions $x=-3,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are  $x=-3,\\,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a602f803-9ee4-55ae-ba4f-d88ab6ec6a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402697} {:answer/id #uuid "d1acf715-1373-5a06-b01b-ab3b303c535d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402698} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbc63c9-13bb-57db-8074-636542eaac49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402699} {:answer/id #uuid "66e7f76d-26a7-52c7-993f-99c8712a83c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402700} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6dfaf-76c0-5356-9336-dc60a8e77b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6dfaf-76c0-5356-9336-dc60a8e77b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402701} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e77536-5db4-551c-9973-483a9864ffa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "10e486fb-a153-567a-b122-83eab72817c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-39936" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}}{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083381 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c17552f7-0720-5e45-9c79-7db73e332045" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Formula for the Sum of a Geometric Series Given the First Few Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18402f06-e7fe-5e53-bc39-a52202724c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}(1-{5}^{N})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324182} {:answer/id #uuid "289b6022-d0c7-5c10-9f15-ede2cefd24ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}(1-{3}^{N})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324297} {:answer/id #uuid "9902e8fc-b922-563d-a9ba-221cda3929a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({5}^{N}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325273} {:answer/id #uuid "d22a6abc-1542-5fcf-b211-a0f1527499e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}({3}^{N}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325796} {:answer/id #uuid "da6e7c75-28b1-5101-9cba-e617b2f59a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}({5}^{N}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da6e7c75-28b1-5101-9cba-e617b2f59a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}({5}^{N}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325873} :answer-field/id #uuid "4140fc40-4e00-5ded-95cf-2f275426a24b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb043af4-ab8f-5c34-af27-840fcf0ff65d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39996" :question/problem "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series $3+15+75+⋯\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the common ratio $r$ for this geometric series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case ${a}_{1}=3$ and $r=5.$ Substituting these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1-{5}^{N})}{1-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1-{5}^{N})}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(1-{5}^{N}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}({5}^{N}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd0aa5fc-79de-525e-a688-a8a2d2fcf858" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant in a Transformed Reciprocal Function Given the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36817ff5-94b5-5e0c-aa6e-e4adfd7b868f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324418} {:answer/id #uuid "58722e71-13e2-5d86-b77b-8b1c37339129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324706} {:answer/id #uuid "796d99a4-c609-5611-b9b4-030174fd4671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324984} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb00e44-4f46-5766-841d-29d6d2a9f5b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325767} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1f23f3-9644-55d1-99f6-e9850f0217bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceb00e44-4f46-5766-841d-29d6d2a9f5b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325767} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a286a71-fa21-5505-b96c-f7267c23eddf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d984b68-ba41-5abd-a3f2-afc368e8283a" :question/math-academy-id "q-40027" :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4}{3x+b}$ is $(-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (-4,\\infty ).$ Find the value of $b.$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+b & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-b \\\\ x & =-\\frac{b}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=-\\frac{b}{3}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=-4$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $-\\frac{b}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{b}{3} & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{b}{3} & =4 \\\\ b & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd0aa5fc-79de-525e-a688-a8a2d2fcf858" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant in a Transformed Reciprocal Function Given the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a1706a0-c6dd-59ae-a43b-e66df8af405c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324306} {:answer/id #uuid "2bda2eaa-5096-56df-8746-32e4920b368f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324321} {:answer/id #uuid "49f7f4a7-34ec-5b9a-8ed0-13e3f1e0b503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324573} {:answer/id #uuid "9abd3c31-5d1e-5960-b4b5-b54b731e88e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325290} {:answer/id #uuid "be709329-41b4-53a2-b1be-7fa2b14e58b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a1706a0-c6dd-59ae-a43b-e66df8af405c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324306} :answer-field/id #uuid "77df51fa-0395-5164-8ce7-6392c30aa09c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c814038d-ed82-5b29-a75e-05c1ef5d3931" :question/math-academy-id "q-40028" :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{6}{ax-18}+5$ is $(-\\infty ,9)\\cup (9,+\\infty ).$ What is the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax-18 & =0 \\\\ ax & =18 \\\\ x & =\\frac{18}{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=\\frac{18}{a}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=9$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $\\frac{18}{a},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18}{a} & =9 \\\\ a & =\\frac{18}{9} \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ad95b4e-e367-5e26-800c-1a87a537ca83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Trigonometric Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3497a718-c1a6-51e6-af1a-953f981abd22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\pi ,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324397} {:answer/id #uuid "40780318-a745-5f1a-8bba-affb853af852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324489} {:answer/id #uuid "7216b300-5c68-553d-ab9a-ac506700f72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-\\pi ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324916} {:answer/id #uuid "a0aeec49-d531-50db-bf21-b8578d86c37d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325333} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d962bb-8ba4-5e15-a962-658461433d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5d962bb-8ba4-5e15-a962-658461433d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325832} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f7faea1-42ca-5601-a0d5-7ae190223c12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14982c8b-d480-564b-82c7-d131ac68638d" :question/math-academy-id "q-40081" :question/problem "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $-\\pi \\le x\\le \\pi .$ What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $g(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ is continuous?" :question/worked-solution "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is continuous over the entire interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ but the function $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=-\\pi ,0,\\pi .$ \n\nTherefore, the domain of $g(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\pi ,0)$ and $(0,\\pi ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133940 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbff5997-1801-5dec-8e3d-e3d1ff309015" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Polynomial Inequality Using a Given Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a11b12-284c-582d-a7a9-2d00ea37a25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-3,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324009} {:answer/id #uuid "88d3b48b-18d3-5306-a2e4-972b4bfad741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325136} {:answer/id #uuid "952ea22a-67e2-5092-a7bb-105455f16e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325241} {:answer/id #uuid "b1805914-ac09-562c-9ac7-a5a2b0eeffd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325486} {:answer/id #uuid "f342f39d-b7f2-5ea1-81be-16a2a53514a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f342f39d-b7f2-5ea1-81be-16a2a53514a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326113} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d01944b-c7b1-551c-9169-238fe36ae750" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07601ac3-801f-55de-9d68-76f24103442d" :question/math-academy-id "q-40185" :question/problem "![](images/62/62502a59f15828ddfe1850039930128c0c382b072504725038ecaac9b9b90196.png)\n\nGiven the sketch of a polynomial $p(x)$ above, find the solution to $p(x)<0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality $p(x)<0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=p(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n![](images/9b/9b283fb0afe6a6f8bf6950765bbcb845c1262b01c278cc7bdca20b6fe14280ff.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133941 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b71f10-5b2b-5cae-a5dc-b9c160484d82" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03307e39-9912-54d0-97d1-4687514485a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324004} {:answer/id #uuid "2f544eb0-303c-5bdf-929f-8cfa78dc27ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324351} {:answer/id #uuid "88c95372-3160-560b-8efc-3d0ea505b8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,0)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325134} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ae322b-2788-5b7a-b210-bf545ea5361a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,1)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325626} {:answer/id #uuid "e18a4049-7e1b-52d2-8d8f-95d82557c997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0ae322b-2788-5b7a-b210-bf545ea5361a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,1)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325626} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeb6213f-88f3-5c1c-9279-b6d7114b6c14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58262a62-5cba-55bc-b202-213c3b519acb" :question/math-academy-id "q-40270" :question/problem "Given the polynomial $P(x)=(x+2)(x-1)(x-2),$ find the solution of $P(x)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=(x+2)(x-1)(x-2)$ are $x=1$ and $x=\\pm 2,$ which are all simple roots.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a cubic polynomial with a positive leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](images/65/65a899ed4375be4681c0e4598cc4769335cabb52257a886148438f4b4f16e671.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-2,1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent Geometric Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58761f09-07f7-57a6-ab8e-19cc89659cdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402718} {:answer/id #uuid "349f551e-f45b-5529-8d98-1ce5d2275f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402719} {:answer/id #uuid "35974d94-069e-5ca4-a79a-f3e613a3f779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402720} {:answer/id #uuid "7591e88a-1a5a-5695-9633-43e83d0bff84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402721} {:answer/id #uuid "0d55611a-ae8c-5fb5-998a-98840ea1adad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7591e88a-1a5a-5695-9633-43e83d0bff84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402721} :answer-field/id #uuid "6140ca09-668b-5dd3-b823-6e3fa39bae3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d6af0ce-2d7e-5e7e-9759-710fbc03ce1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-40277" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\nI. $4+3+\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{16}+⋯$\nII. $16-2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{32}+⋯$\nIII. $-8+16-32+64-⋯$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $4+3+\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{16}+⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $16-2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{32}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{-2}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $-8+16-32+64-⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{16}{-8} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series I and II converge."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48de9cc7-5f86-51e4-a827-796f77381af2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Polynomial Inequality Containing No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c3ec426-1ea7-5331-ae61-2ed49607e576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-1,0)\\cup (0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324325} {:answer/id #uuid "34bb6b07-1f01-55c8-896f-35270b61f469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324399} {:answer/id #uuid "5c28c8e9-bdb0-54ad-9b8f-f10de0d87a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324733} {:answer/id #uuid "81d61567-9859-5557-b079-5911bf3c8178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325062} {:answer/id #uuid "8f296f29-1b48-5f27-ab90-dbf96c0b4dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34bb6b07-1f01-55c8-896f-35270b61f469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324399} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3bc4614-1e0e-5f10-a459-30b472ac84aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00233489-491b-51f0-b8aa-31a9f55880aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-40334" :question/problem "Given $P(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2},$ solve $P(x)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we factorize the polynomial $P(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & ={x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-x-2) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+x-2x-2) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x+1)-2(x+1)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+1)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}$ are $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=2,$ where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=-1$ and $x=2$ are simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c0756761d00b0805683e447d8cee3b657bbbe004a0a47da428927585037b06c2.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c80b75e7-19b2-534b-97f8-aba563ebe058" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Cubic Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b2e8b63-81e7-5a1c-a343-d2e83bac41de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324201} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4cf899-ff99-50a7-ab03-977e0a939e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324597} {:answer/id #uuid "849305c9-d929-518e-bf23-35902ab0355f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325088} {:answer/id #uuid "be244597-d382-5777-9a00-d13314a385f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325604} {:answer/id #uuid "fa451380-973c-50c6-af68-c706839c4054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "849305c9-d929-518e-bf23-35902ab0355f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325088} :answer-field/id #uuid "2359e231-cc4e-518b-8b28-8c3c01996302" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f08a6c50-6d74-51fe-a849-876f9c4eebbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-40347" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $(x+2)(2x-1)(x-3)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)(2x-1)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-2,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (3,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e675f1c-9093-5f6e-a3b3-112d0bc8e516" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Higher-Order Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6907f133-726d-5295-9a65-8baa32dad0ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324847} {:answer/id #uuid "bfba6bb4-f731-5f93-9272-ca3090183685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [3,9]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325620} {:answer/id #uuid "e25b3f45-f7a4-54d0-861f-a58d58bf78a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325955} {:answer/id #uuid "ea863e41-66ae-543b-b91a-f33c8016ca22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-9]\\cup [9,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326038} {:answer/id #uuid "f5af357d-7351-5ca2-aabe-7dbe9c884c16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-9,9]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6907f133-726d-5295-9a65-8baa32dad0ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324847} :answer-field/id #uuid "42a8a8b0-3024-5b9d-8918-75295d52687f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f7db4ba5-260a-5399-a20a-525307eb753a" :question/math-academy-id "q-40368" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $({x}^{2}+3)(x+3)(x-3)\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=({x}^{2}+3)(x+3)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-3$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-3),\\,(-3,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+3$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [3,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b2ac075-4b9b-52d3-b053-84fe2d1b6545" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Series Given in Sigma Notation Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0a0d5ce-ea74-563c-8226-ef12abfcf57c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402725} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca6c567-4159-5b02-87ac-496a7d90cc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402726} {:answer/id #uuid "af2e493c-45e0-51f4-9bd1-9e4936ea0db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402727} {:answer/id #uuid "419f3375-7f3d-502c-ad8d-fae3914d69e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402728} {:answer/id #uuid "ef235626-3ba2-5405-8b4e-45af49053e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|<1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ca6c567-4159-5b02-87ac-496a7d90cc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402726} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc63abdb-8696-5444-ac6a-673734bc4695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ecdef7ff-1e8e-5448-88b3-81c3a4f39ef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-40579" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }0.12{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3n}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.12{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3(2)}}{0.12{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3(1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{6}}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092352 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb05e50-e916-5af9-9de3-557a92adafe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324078} {:answer/id #uuid "15f5b0e5-36f4-5e3f-885f-1546a9fdb085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324165} {:answer/id #uuid "775eb3e6-28ee-5f28-bfba-580e5657ca4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324963} {:answer/id #uuid "e778fe47-604a-5d4b-8e1c-09fadfa8ae14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326010} {:answer/id #uuid "fe16e149-182d-50c4-8fde-64ba1009bfa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe16e149-182d-50c4-8fde-64ba1009bfa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326213} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cc3f45c-0d6a-5e58-a3b5-a1d1a8240ecf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea730d8c-46db-5efe-9ba8-729d43d49a6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-40756" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(0,12).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the positive half of $y$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(12,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4034e61-63dc-5f72-8275-e9c81290767d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Polar Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daf34dcd-691c-5756-a4ac-48595ad3a189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bf1a9b27d362e8f44736e7210836537eb2d05e760cf654bb3da332dc2da9fd1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392377} {:answer/id #uuid "1239b38f-9435-5446-937d-574946b68942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bb98fa6cae9226c4334773a013fe7ac0749174e1241442946aa727b9c96d0015.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392378} {:answer/id #uuid "986036ad-70fa-5556-92fe-72217fe9bc3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/16/161e6fe0bc44415a26a2a75f78ed44e8fbadf3ec0e56773dde8c61a15ba0b035.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392379} {:answer/id #uuid "45f27d07-4089-57d8-aa8a-88d48e6b9472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/da/dad45fcb266c0313a1b02d08eb93763bc00c103d4a1d4da48f2ef5452a27dd61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392380} {:answer/id #uuid "5d50c7dc-6078-540f-a255-a7009d5d11fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ea/ea7595da12f74310da86ccf44db453dadf67ccff889aaf562f461f7c7e713807.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "986036ad-70fa-5556-92fe-72217fe9bc3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/16/161e6fe0bc44415a26a2a75f78ed44e8fbadf3ec0e56773dde8c61a15ba0b035.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392379} :answer-field/id #uuid "43a6c6de-17d3-5232-b82b-db818cc48537" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f87f6e7-836d-57de-ad54-3c3ac690c6db" :question/math-academy-id "q-40790" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r=8?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation $r=8$ is a circle of radius $8$ centered at the origin. Its graph is given below.\n\n![](images/16/161e6fe0bc44415a26a2a75f78ed44e8fbadf3ec0e56773dde8c61a15ba0b035.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4034e61-63dc-5f72-8275-e9c81290767d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Polar Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "287b66ce-eea9-5761-8ae3-e433ccc705bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324296} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ad43de-68fe-5a9c-aea2-663552beff50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325393} {:answer/id #uuid "b8aea7da-f08f-5e62-9eb6-3d6d90b0ef32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325556} {:answer/id #uuid "c1392b6e-aa07-545b-9154-c8ff037361cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325631} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dd1117-4024-55d3-ab48-87b95cec4c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4dd1117-4024-55d3-ab48-87b95cec4c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325666} :answer-field/id #uuid "733270aa-9c3b-5f1e-a50b-48efd158ae2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ebf40886-fb73-5481-a019-3b75d93ee8ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-40791" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d5c95dca34605acc8f66ca24f11e8d048ed3490ae859c55e7f5eb42806307071.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is a circle of radius $6$ centered at the origin.\n\nTherefore, its polar equation is $r=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given Some Information About Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de63e38-80fb-54c4-9a7c-5a96600b508f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},4\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324222} {:answer/id #uuid "23d41aa2-c3a0-5270-abba-3a1f3207c3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324266} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4dd8b9-9d94-588f-a6d0-7d91017750d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324340} {:answer/id #uuid "81916b05-53d5-5cfe-b7c6-b0642b2c06ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{3},8\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325056} {:answer/id #uuid "82355246-8fcd-5520-a5e5-83b3cb9b05c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{2},8\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23d41aa2-c3a0-5270-abba-3a1f3207c3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324266} :answer-field/id #uuid "3036c726-f5d4-51ab-a7e4-77d619c6072b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "417c4b2b-c91e-563b-b92c-41495ea7b2b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-40826" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(8,\\theta +\\pi ).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(8,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+\\pi )=(8,\\frac{9\\pi }{4})=(8,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "407616a9-e448-5652-9882-a1edf19600f8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Radial Line Given Its Polar Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "558d39e0-0ab6-5d33-b3a0-396e26bb8653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/aa/aa72b03a85b16e3a9ef9dc3f65f4126cdee0339ac722fcfe8a1f21973796f623.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392384} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd87d8b-270f-573d-9412-5025cd44dba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/10d1cdb8ecbfe7f7153618b5da57fecefbf8711d395cf3c6f5c879f3169977f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392385} {:answer/id #uuid "97873c7d-0308-59b1-889c-345b9fb3b8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/802bb767b28c38598663d928d66338086adf08b80bc5887a7795704183ed2212.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392386} {:answer/id #uuid "bee250d6-c6dd-5d05-9b3d-34683cc111c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/85271401b413b162990a99e905ebecbfb4048953e77bcb5808c0b729c1fc7de3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392387} {:answer/id #uuid "521ffe4f-8b8b-5b4e-afbd-79c02578ee0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2b/2b88d001cbc59e0c46922b6e369644e50a02eff26c800e9d571147988357918e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97873c7d-0308-59b1-889c-345b9fb3b8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/802bb767b28c38598663d928d66338086adf08b80bc5887a7795704183ed2212.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392386} :answer-field/id #uuid "93203f91-1d26-5d83-a83e-32f479d20351" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15ae4fee-42a8-584b-a59a-68427466f591" :question/math-academy-id "q-40828" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve  $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{10}?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{10}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\frac{11\\pi }{10}$ with the positive $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the angle it makes with the negative $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\frac{11\\pi }{10}-\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe correct answer is shown below.\n\n![](images/80/802bb767b28c38598663d928d66338086adf08b80bc5887a7795704183ed2212.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129102 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd6703d2-36f6-5665-90e3-f75aea5f663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Expression for the Domain of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56259cef-6878-51cd-b1f5-eeaf49702140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324688} {:answer/id #uuid "654e271d-49cd-5e3c-9c7d-b37514a25a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324810} {:answer/id #uuid "8b458fe6-2549-577d-823d-3fcf10021ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325153} {:answer/id #uuid "d136e134-3728-57dd-b18c-c29e658d49ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325789} {:answer/id #uuid "fd810710-4cf3-54c8-88a7-9403d3f91aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{n\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "654e271d-49cd-5e3c-9c7d-b37514a25a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324810} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff7a4b9e-8f3f-5b3c-98a8-33205f758cf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a89fb0a0-3a36-552f-b729-6c5581cebcd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-41106" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ 3x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3\\cdot 3}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115202 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc0be03-9369-508e-9765-8c88871ec20b" :knowledge-point/title "The Range of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87c6f1e6-0d33-5cdc-af67-5f19a9b4678a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325127} {:answer/id #uuid "cb12b821-bce4-53ba-809b-8073091be039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325732} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3e7d3c-28cb-551b-814d-24c71777a04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325772} {:answer/id #uuid "dbdb2d32-06a5-584c-b837-e088b83a23f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325886} {:answer/id #uuid "eb32a561-e406-5ef8-b8f6-32579968df6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87c6f1e6-0d33-5cdc-af67-5f19a9b4678a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325127} :answer-field/id #uuid "14aade30-4dcf-54fe-90ad-21a8d183cf5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d356ad64-d32b-5d91-870e-d983fd54ca81" :question/math-academy-id "q-41240" :question/problem "Determine the range of the function $f(x)=2{log}_{5}⁡(2x-1)+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y={log}_{5}⁡(x)$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}]}
